WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.964
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:13.350 --> 00:00:15.760
Good afternoon, and welcome.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:20.180
I'm Kim Vandiver, the
chairman of the MIT faculty.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.150
We gather here today
to honor two people--

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:27.635
James R Killian, Jr. and
Professor Noam Chomsky.

00:00:30.370 --> 00:00:33.580
The Killian Award
is the highest award

00:00:33.580 --> 00:00:38.970
that the MIT faculty
bestows upon one of its own,

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:42.970
named after the late Jim
Killian, 10th president of MIT.

00:00:45.580 --> 00:00:50.320
He came to MIT as a
student in the mid-1920s.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.970
He joined the staff of
Technology Review in 1926,

00:00:54.970 --> 00:00:58.940
and he retired as chairman
of the MIT Corporation

00:00:58.940 --> 00:01:04.519
45 years later, in 1971.

00:01:04.519 --> 00:01:08.690
He brought great honor
and distinction to MIT

00:01:08.690 --> 00:01:11.960
through his many years
of national service.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.510
I'll mention two
examples, which are just

00:01:14.510 --> 00:01:15.560
the tip of the iceberg.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:22.260
In the late 1950s, he was the
first full-time science adviser

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:25.050
to a president of
the United States,

00:01:25.050 --> 00:01:27.570
and provided
important leadership

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:30.142
in the period that set
the nation's course

00:01:30.142 --> 00:01:31.350
to putting a man on the moon.

00:01:34.020 --> 00:01:37.860
He also served as chairman
of the Carnegie Commission

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:41.730
on Educational Television,
which recommended

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:45.167
the formation of the Corporation
for Public Broadcasting.

00:01:47.850 --> 00:01:52.270
He was also a very genuine
and likeable person,

00:01:52.270 --> 00:01:55.570
as I discovered quite
by accident one day

00:01:55.570 --> 00:02:01.640
about 20 years ago, when I was
a new graduate student at MIT.

00:02:01.640 --> 00:02:05.240
I sat down at a table in
the Lobdell dining room

00:02:05.240 --> 00:02:08.750
with an elderly gentleman
that I didn't know,

00:02:08.750 --> 00:02:11.870
and we struck up a
conversation, one

00:02:11.870 --> 00:02:16.100
that was to begin a long series
of enlightening conversations

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:21.190
I had with him until the
time of his death in 1988.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:25.030
His easy way with students and
with other members of the MIT

00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:31.260
community are one of the things
that define Jim Killian to us.

00:02:31.260 --> 00:02:34.770
This award is
named in his honor.

00:02:34.770 --> 00:02:36.550
We bestow it upon
a faculty member

00:02:36.550 --> 00:02:42.055
each year who has also brought
honor and distinction to MIT.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:49.220
Professor Chomsky has
been doing so for 37 years

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:52.820
as a member of our faculty.

00:02:52.820 --> 00:02:57.290
Professor Chomsky, would you
come to the stage, please?

00:02:57.290 --> 00:03:00.770
In preparing for today, I did
one small piece of research

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:05.340
on Professor Chomsky, which
I'd like to share with you.

00:03:05.340 --> 00:03:09.130
With the help of the
MIT library staff,

00:03:09.130 --> 00:03:14.400
I was able to determine how
often he is cited in the Arts

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:16.625
and Humanities Citation Index.

00:03:16.625 --> 00:03:19.360
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:23.170
As of the last time the
statistics were compiled,

00:03:23.170 --> 00:03:27.130
we discovered that
he is in the top 10.

00:03:27.130 --> 00:03:30.040
It happens that none of the
others are still living.

00:03:30.040 --> 00:03:32.956
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:33.930 --> 00:03:38.260
He is the most
cited living author.

00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:41.170
I thought you might like to
know the names of the company he

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:42.730
keeps.

00:03:42.730 --> 00:03:44.290
They are, in order--

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:46.300
Marx, Lennon--

00:03:46.300 --> 00:03:49.210
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:52.120
[APPLAUSE]

00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:56.300
Shakespeare--

00:03:56.300 --> 00:03:58.380
[LAUGHTER]

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.110
Aristotle, the Bible--

00:04:01.110 --> 00:04:02.880
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:08.510
Plato, Freud, Chomsky,
followed by Hegel and Cicero.

00:04:08.510 --> 00:04:11.414
[LAUGHTER]

00:04:11.414 --> 00:04:14.318
[APPLAUSE]

00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:24.970
I would like to
read from the award.

00:04:24.970 --> 00:04:27.520
It says, "The president and
faculty of the Massachusetts

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:29.830
Institute of Technology
have the honor

00:04:29.830 --> 00:04:33.970
to present the James R Killian,
Jr. Faculty Achievement Award

00:04:33.970 --> 00:04:37.660
to Noam Chomsky, and to announce
his appointment as Killian

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.880
Award Lecturer for
the academic year

00:04:39.880 --> 00:04:44.890
'91, '92, in recognition of
his extraordinary contributions

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:49.030
to linguistics, philosophy,
and cognitive psychology.

00:04:49.030 --> 00:04:52.030
One of the foremost
thinkers of this century,

00:04:52.030 --> 00:04:56.650
he revolutionized the study of
language and of the human mind.

00:04:56.650 --> 00:04:58.780
His books, monographs,
and articles

00:04:58.780 --> 00:05:04.070
constitute major milestones
in the history of linguistics.

00:05:04.070 --> 00:05:06.350
An inspiring teacher,
he has trained

00:05:06.350 --> 00:05:09.980
scholars who are among the
most important contributors

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:11.480
to the discipline.

00:05:11.480 --> 00:05:16.140
His presence here has
greatly enriched MIT."

00:05:16.140 --> 00:05:18.478
Professor Chomsky, the
microphone is yours.

00:05:18.478 --> 00:05:21.466
[APPLAUSE]

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:42.564
He's lucky he didn't read what
those citations say, but--

00:05:42.564 --> 00:05:45.516
[LAUGHTER]

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:49.320
The title of this talk,
the announced title,

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:51.600
has three parts--

00:05:51.600 --> 00:05:56.880
refers to language, the
cognitive revolutions.

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:01.740
And then there's the quotes
around cognitive revolutions.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:06.870
The relation between language
and cognitive science

00:06:06.870 --> 00:06:08.130
is natural.

00:06:08.130 --> 00:06:11.070
That's a reasonable combination.

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:13.380
Modern linguistics
developed as part

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:16.170
of, what's sometimes called,
the cognitive revolution

00:06:16.170 --> 00:06:17.850
of the 1950s--

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:21.320
actually to a large extent
here in building 20,

00:06:21.320 --> 00:06:25.590
thanks initially to Jerry
Wiesner's initiatives.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:29.130
It was also a major
factor in the development

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:34.210
of the cognitive sciences ever
since their modern origins.

00:06:34.210 --> 00:06:37.660
So those two words
are sort of obvious.

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:43.380
The quotes are intended to
express a lingering skepticism.

00:06:43.380 --> 00:06:47.760
And when I say cognitive
revolution from here on in,

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:52.150
you're supposed to hear a
so-called cognitive revolution.

00:06:52.150 --> 00:06:55.650
I'll explain why as I proceed.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:59.790
The skepticism that I feel
does not have anything

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:02.250
to do with the
actual work that's

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:06.400
done in the various areas
of cognitive science--

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:12.300
so vision, language, cognitive
development, a few others.

00:07:12.300 --> 00:07:18.120
In these areas, there have
been quite significant advances

00:07:18.120 --> 00:07:20.400
over the past 40 years.

00:07:20.400 --> 00:07:24.510
The questions in my mind
arise at a second order level,

00:07:24.510 --> 00:07:27.780
that is, thinking about the
nature of the disciplines that

00:07:27.780 --> 00:07:31.830
are concerned with
what were traditionally

00:07:31.830 --> 00:07:35.094
called mental acts
or mental faculties.

00:07:35.094 --> 00:07:37.260
And I want to come back to
a few of those questions.

00:07:37.260 --> 00:07:38.468
Some of them are substantive.

00:07:38.468 --> 00:07:40.350
Some of them are historical.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:45.840
First, a few words about the
contemporary cognitive sciences

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:50.190
and the cognitive revolution,
as they look to me.

00:07:50.190 --> 00:07:54.510
And let me stress that others
don't see it this way at all.

00:07:54.510 --> 00:07:58.350
This is not intended to be a
survey, but rather a rather

00:07:58.350 --> 00:08:01.920
personal point of view.

00:08:01.920 --> 00:08:04.620
George Miller, who was
one of the leading figures

00:08:04.620 --> 00:08:11.190
and founders of the modern
field, in a recent article,

00:08:11.190 --> 00:08:16.860
he traced the modern
cognitive sciences back

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:21.720
to a meeting in 1956,
held in Cambridge.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.730
I can't remember whether
it was here or at Harvard,

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:26.880
but one or the other.

00:08:26.880 --> 00:08:31.870
It was a meeting of the
Institute of Radio Engineers,

00:08:31.870 --> 00:08:38.190
which had a series of papers on
experimental human psychology,

00:08:38.190 --> 00:08:42.750
which made use of information
theory and signal detection,

00:08:42.750 --> 00:08:45.560
and other then pretty new ideas.

00:08:45.560 --> 00:08:48.360
It had a paper of
mine on language,

00:08:48.360 --> 00:08:52.230
which outlined some of the basic
ideas of generative grammar.

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:57.420
It had another paper on
problem-solving and reasoning.

00:08:57.420 --> 00:09:01.200
It was a paper by Herb
Simon and Al Newell,

00:09:01.200 --> 00:09:06.240
a simulation of theorem
proving in elementary logic.

00:09:06.240 --> 00:09:11.410
And he argues that that
confluence of research papers

00:09:11.410 --> 00:09:14.530
essentially initiated
the modern field.

00:09:14.530 --> 00:09:20.320
Well, there were some shared
ideas among the participants.

00:09:20.320 --> 00:09:25.630
One shared idea was a kind
of shift in perception

00:09:25.630 --> 00:09:29.710
or perspective toward the
disciplines, a shift which

00:09:29.710 --> 00:09:32.470
is not terribly surprising
now, but was pretty striking

00:09:32.470 --> 00:09:34.360
at that moment.

00:09:34.360 --> 00:09:38.800
The shift was away from
the study of behavior

00:09:38.800 --> 00:09:42.940
and the product of behavior,
such as words or sounds

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:45.520
or their arrangement
in text, a shift

00:09:45.520 --> 00:09:50.380
from that focus toward the study
of the states and properties

00:09:50.380 --> 00:09:54.820
of the mind and the brain
that enter into human thought

00:09:54.820 --> 00:09:56.200
and action.

00:09:56.200 --> 00:09:59.710
From the first point of view,
which overwhelmingly prevailed

00:09:59.710 --> 00:10:03.010
at the time, behavior
and its products

00:10:03.010 --> 00:10:05.860
were regarded as
the object of study.

00:10:05.860 --> 00:10:08.500
From the second
point of view, what

00:10:08.500 --> 00:10:11.230
later came to be called
the cognitive sciences,

00:10:11.230 --> 00:10:14.650
behavior and its products
were of no particular interest

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:15.850
in themselves.

00:10:15.850 --> 00:10:21.060
They simply provided data,
data which would be interesting

00:10:21.060 --> 00:10:25.810
insofar as it served as evidence
for what really concerns us

00:10:25.810 --> 00:10:29.230
now, the inner workings of
the mind, where the phrase

00:10:29.230 --> 00:10:32.890
mind here simply
is a way of talking

00:10:32.890 --> 00:10:35.440
about relevant
properties and states

00:10:35.440 --> 00:10:38.410
and processes of the brain.

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:41.530
The data of behavior
and the text,

00:10:41.530 --> 00:10:43.060
and so on, from
this point of view,

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:46.570
are to be used alongside
of other data, whatever

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:48.520
might turn out to
be useful, say,

00:10:48.520 --> 00:10:51.020
electrical activity
of the brain.

00:10:51.020 --> 00:10:53.840
But it has no inherently
privileged status,

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:55.810
and it's not the
focus of interest.

00:10:55.810 --> 00:10:56.770
That's a shift.

00:10:56.770 --> 00:10:58.900
It's a shift away
from what was called

00:10:58.900 --> 00:11:01.110
behavioral science,
or structuralism,

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:04.870
or stressed statistical
analysis of texts,

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:07.270
and a shift toward something
that might properly

00:11:07.270 --> 00:11:09.280
be called cognitive science.

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:11.290
In my personal
view, it was a shift

00:11:11.290 --> 00:11:14.410
from a kind of natural
history to at least

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:16.600
potential natural science.

00:11:16.600 --> 00:11:20.110
Now, that shift was highly
controversial at the time,

00:11:20.110 --> 00:11:22.040
and it still is.

00:11:22.040 --> 00:11:26.590
And in fact, if you were to
use such measures as amount

00:11:26.590 --> 00:11:29.110
of government funding,
it would still

00:11:29.110 --> 00:11:31.660
be very much in the minority.

00:11:31.660 --> 00:11:34.070
But I think it was
the right move.

00:11:34.070 --> 00:11:37.180
Well, that was one
kind of shared interest

00:11:37.180 --> 00:11:38.660
from independent ones of view.

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:40.780
There was no communication
among the people

00:11:40.780 --> 00:11:42.120
prior to the meeting.

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:44.890
A second shared
interest was an interest

00:11:44.890 --> 00:11:48.280
in what we might call
computational representational

00:11:48.280 --> 00:11:50.410
systems.

00:11:50.410 --> 00:11:53.350
That means a way of looking
at mental activities

00:11:53.350 --> 00:11:55.860
and mental faculties, at
what the brain is doing,

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:58.930
to looking at them as a kind
of a software problem, that

00:11:58.930 --> 00:12:01.900
is, a study of the
mechanisms, but viewed

00:12:01.900 --> 00:12:05.590
from a particular
abstract perspective.

00:12:05.590 --> 00:12:08.710
Analogies can always
be misleading,

00:12:08.710 --> 00:12:12.260
but one might compare this
approach to, say, chemistry

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:16.150
about a century ago, where
there were notions like valence

00:12:16.150 --> 00:12:20.410
or periodic table or
organic molecule, and so on,

00:12:20.410 --> 00:12:25.042
but they were not grounded in
the basic physics of the time.

00:12:25.042 --> 00:12:26.500
The results, however,
and the ideas

00:12:26.500 --> 00:12:29.980
did provide guidelines
for what turned out

00:12:29.980 --> 00:12:32.290
to be quite radical
changes in physics

00:12:32.290 --> 00:12:35.890
that were necessary to
accommodate these ideas.

00:12:35.890 --> 00:12:38.980
Chemistry was never
actually reduced to physics.

00:12:38.980 --> 00:12:41.080
It would be more accurate
to say that physics

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:44.800
was expanded, in a certain
sense, to chemistry.

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:48.460
Similarly, computation
representational theories

00:12:48.460 --> 00:12:54.370
of the brain attempt to
establish states and properties

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:57.910
that have to be accommodated
somehow in the brain sciences,

00:12:57.910 --> 00:13:01.030
and might well provide,
insofar as they're correct,

00:13:01.030 --> 00:13:03.490
they might well provide
guidelines for inquiry

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:05.090
into mechanisms.

00:13:05.090 --> 00:13:08.380
It's possible, again, that
they may provide guidelines

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:13.180
for what will have to be
radical changes in the way

00:13:13.180 --> 00:13:18.280
that the brain and its processes
and states are conceived.

00:13:18.280 --> 00:13:21.310
How two approaches
to the natural world

00:13:21.310 --> 00:13:25.750
will be unified, if
ever, can never be

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:27.550
guessed in advance really.

00:13:27.550 --> 00:13:32.500
It could be reduction, as in,
say, the biology and chemistry

00:13:32.500 --> 00:13:33.670
case, more or less.

00:13:33.670 --> 00:13:35.890
It could be what you
might call expansion,

00:13:35.890 --> 00:13:38.020
as in the physics,
chemistry case,

00:13:38.020 --> 00:13:40.090
or it could be something else.

00:13:40.090 --> 00:13:43.540
Crucially, there's nothing
mysterious about any of this.

00:13:43.540 --> 00:13:46.990
It seems, to me at least,
to be just normal science,

00:13:46.990 --> 00:13:50.770
normal science at a typical
stage before unification

00:13:50.770 --> 00:13:56.170
of several approaches through
some topic in the natural world

00:13:56.170 --> 00:13:58.180
before they're unified.

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:02.260
Well, again, that was and
remains highly controversial.

00:14:02.260 --> 00:14:05.230
Assuming it, anyway,
how can we proceed?

00:14:05.230 --> 00:14:07.930
You can look at the
brain or the person

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:10.010
from a number of points of view.

00:14:10.010 --> 00:14:12.260
Let's begin by
taking what you might

00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:14.310
call a receptive point of view.

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:18.040
So we think of the brain, say,
as being in a certain state.

00:14:18.040 --> 00:14:20.600
A signal reaches the brain.

00:14:20.600 --> 00:14:23.810
It produces some kind of
symbolic representation

00:14:23.810 --> 00:14:26.390
of the signal
internally, presumably,

00:14:26.390 --> 00:14:30.110
and it moves to
some other state.

00:14:30.110 --> 00:14:33.530
If we focus on the
interstate transitions,

00:14:33.530 --> 00:14:35.630
we're looking at
problems of learning

00:14:35.630 --> 00:14:38.150
or maybe, more
accurately, growth.

00:14:38.150 --> 00:14:42.620
If we focus on the input signal
and the symbolic representation

00:14:42.620 --> 00:14:46.010
of it, we're looking
at perception.

00:14:46.010 --> 00:14:49.790
If we focus on the initial
state of the system

00:14:49.790 --> 00:14:53.000
prior to any relevant
signals, we're

00:14:53.000 --> 00:14:55.100
looking at the
biological endowment,

00:14:55.100 --> 00:14:56.570
the innate structure.

00:14:56.570 --> 00:14:59.620
We could trace the
processes back further.

00:14:59.620 --> 00:15:02.930
At that point, we'd be
moving into embryology.

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:05.630
And one should stress that
these distinctions are

00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:08.090
pretty artificial, actually.

00:15:08.090 --> 00:15:10.760
Typically, the systems
that we're looking at

00:15:10.760 --> 00:15:12.410
are like physical organs.

00:15:12.410 --> 00:15:16.790
Or we might say more accurately,
like other physical organs,

00:15:16.790 --> 00:15:18.500
because we are studying
a physical organ

00:15:18.500 --> 00:15:19.833
from a particular point of view.

00:15:22.800 --> 00:15:26.270
They are like physical organs,
other physical organs, in that,

00:15:26.270 --> 00:15:29.600
say, like the digestive system
or the circulatory system

00:15:29.600 --> 00:15:32.160
or the visual
system, or whatever,

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:35.420
which, again, we often
look at abstractly,

00:15:35.420 --> 00:15:37.640
as one would expect in
the study of the system

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:39.680
of any degree of complexity.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:42.950
We can call these particular
systems mental organs,

00:15:42.950 --> 00:15:46.010
not implying, again,
anything more than

00:15:46.010 --> 00:15:49.220
that they are involved in
what were traditionally

00:15:49.220 --> 00:15:53.600
called mental states, mental
faculties, or mental processes.

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:56.730
Well, like other
physical organs,

00:15:56.730 --> 00:15:59.000
these mental organs
quite typically

00:15:59.000 --> 00:16:02.180
reach a kind of a stable
state at a certain point.

00:16:02.180 --> 00:16:05.450
That is, the input
signal that's causing

00:16:05.450 --> 00:16:08.810
an interstate transition
moves the system around

00:16:08.810 --> 00:16:11.510
within some kind of equivalence
class of states, that are

00:16:11.510 --> 00:16:14.810
alike, for purposes at hand.

00:16:14.810 --> 00:16:17.270
That's obviously
an idealization,

00:16:17.270 --> 00:16:20.060
but a reasonable one.

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:22.040
When you look at
the stable state

00:16:22.040 --> 00:16:25.220
after the point at which there
isn't a significant change,

00:16:25.220 --> 00:16:26.660
then you're very
clearly studying

00:16:26.660 --> 00:16:32.130
perception, when you look at the
signal or result relationship.

00:16:32.130 --> 00:16:35.270
So for example, if you
take the visual system,

00:16:35.270 --> 00:16:36.830
if you look at
the initial state,

00:16:36.830 --> 00:16:39.170
the state prior to
relevant signals,

00:16:39.170 --> 00:16:42.340
you're looking at the basic
structure of the visual system.

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:44.900
If you look at the
interstate transition,

00:16:44.900 --> 00:16:47.420
you're looking at the
growth of the visual system,

00:16:47.420 --> 00:16:51.590
the changes in the distribution
of vertical and horizontal

00:16:51.590 --> 00:16:55.910
receptors in the striate cortex
or the onset of binocular

00:16:55.910 --> 00:17:00.030
vision after a couple of months
of age in humans, and so on.

00:17:00.030 --> 00:17:02.420
If you look at a
stable state, you're

00:17:02.420 --> 00:17:04.920
studying adult perception.

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:07.970
Adult perception, of course,
has some kind of access

00:17:07.970 --> 00:17:10.920
to systems of belief
about the world.

00:17:10.920 --> 00:17:13.220
Not much is known
about this, but it

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:15.500
seems reasonable to
assume that that involves

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:17.930
some kind of
computational procedure

00:17:17.930 --> 00:17:21.980
that specifies an infinite range
of beliefs, which are called

00:17:21.980 --> 00:17:25.160
upon when needed,
although here, we

00:17:25.160 --> 00:17:29.480
move into a domain of
almost total ignorance.

00:17:29.480 --> 00:17:31.310
Turning to the
language system, you

00:17:31.310 --> 00:17:33.380
can look at it the same way.

00:17:33.380 --> 00:17:36.440
The initial state of the
system, the initial state

00:17:36.440 --> 00:17:40.010
of the language
faculty, the study of it

00:17:40.010 --> 00:17:43.100
is sometimes called universal
grammar, these days.

00:17:43.100 --> 00:17:47.240
The study of the initial
state of the language faculty

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:51.500
is the study of that, is a
study of biological endowment.

00:17:51.500 --> 00:17:54.650
It's an empirical fact, which
seems pretty well established,

00:17:54.650 --> 00:17:59.240
that this element of
the brain is pretty

00:17:59.240 --> 00:18:01.060
uniform across the species.

00:18:01.060 --> 00:18:04.760
The variations appear to be
quite slight apart from extreme

00:18:04.760 --> 00:18:05.880
pathology.

00:18:05.880 --> 00:18:08.750
And it also appears to
be unique in essentials.

00:18:08.750 --> 00:18:12.170
There's no other known organism
that is anything like it.

00:18:12.170 --> 00:18:14.840
Also, it's rather different
from other biological systems

00:18:14.840 --> 00:18:16.310
in many respects.

00:18:16.310 --> 00:18:19.550
Systems of discrete infinity,
which this plainly is,

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:21.650
are pretty rare in
the biological world,

00:18:21.650 --> 00:18:22.400
so it appears.

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:27.830
If you're looking at the
interstate transition

00:18:27.830 --> 00:18:31.010
as signals, some relevant
information comes in,

00:18:31.010 --> 00:18:33.830
you're studying
language acquisition.

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:38.630
It apparently reaches a
stable state at about puberty.

00:18:38.630 --> 00:18:41.750
This stable, steady
state incorporates

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:45.080
a computational procedure,
which characterizes

00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:48.320
an infinite set of
expressions, each

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:51.890
of them having a certain
array of properties,

00:18:51.890 --> 00:18:53.810
properties of sound,
properties of meaning,

00:18:53.810 --> 00:18:56.540
properties of
structural organization.

00:18:56.540 --> 00:19:00.380
That computational procedure
constitutes person's language.

00:19:00.380 --> 00:19:05.000
To have such a procedure in the
appropriate part of the brain

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:07.580
is to have a language
or, as we sometimes

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:09.300
say, to know a language.

00:19:09.300 --> 00:19:11.610
When I speak of a
language, I simply

00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:14.640
mean the computational
or generative procedure

00:19:14.640 --> 00:19:18.030
that specifies the properties
of an infinite range

00:19:18.030 --> 00:19:20.100
of linguistic
expressions, such as, say,

00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:21.780
the one I just produced.

00:19:21.780 --> 00:19:23.850
Since your language
is similar enough

00:19:23.850 --> 00:19:27.390
to mine, when the signal
that I just produced

00:19:27.390 --> 00:19:30.180
reaches your brain, your
generative procedure

00:19:30.180 --> 00:19:33.210
constructs an
expression, an expression

00:19:33.210 --> 00:19:36.720
with these properties, which
is similar enough to the one

00:19:36.720 --> 00:19:41.970
that I form, so we understand
one another, more or less.

00:19:41.970 --> 00:19:44.520
Unlike the system
of belief that's

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:47.500
accessed in visual
perception, in this case,

00:19:47.500 --> 00:19:50.460
we happen to know quite a lot
about the generative procedures

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:52.620
that are involved in
the use of language,

00:19:52.620 --> 00:19:55.740
and even about their
innate, invariant basis.

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:57.660
And these, in
fact, are the areas

00:19:57.660 --> 00:20:00.720
of major progress in
the past generation.

00:20:00.720 --> 00:20:02.580
Well, what about
perception of language?

00:20:06.510 --> 00:20:09.660
There are several common
assumptions about this.

00:20:09.660 --> 00:20:11.550
One major assumption
is that there

00:20:11.550 --> 00:20:13.280
is something called a parser.

00:20:13.280 --> 00:20:16.350
A parser is something
that maps a signal

00:20:16.350 --> 00:20:21.360
into a symbolic representation,
paying no attention

00:20:21.360 --> 00:20:26.460
to any other aspects of
the relevant environment,

00:20:26.460 --> 00:20:28.470
of the environment.

00:20:28.470 --> 00:20:31.110
Of course, it is assumed,
if people are rational,

00:20:31.110 --> 00:20:34.680
that this parser accesses
the generative procedure.

00:20:34.680 --> 00:20:38.010
That is, it's assumed that you
use your knowledge of English

00:20:38.010 --> 00:20:40.530
when you interpret signals.

00:20:40.530 --> 00:20:43.140
Note, however,
several assumptions.

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:45.180
First, one assumption
is that such a thing

00:20:45.180 --> 00:20:47.130
exists, that a parser exists.

00:20:47.130 --> 00:20:49.500
There is some faculty
of the mind that

00:20:49.500 --> 00:20:52.740
interprets signals independently
of any other features

00:20:52.740 --> 00:20:53.970
of the environment.

00:20:53.970 --> 00:20:56.190
That's not at all obvious.

00:20:56.190 --> 00:21:00.600
The existence of an internal,
generative procedure

00:21:00.600 --> 00:21:04.710
is far more secure and far
more obvious than the question

00:21:04.710 --> 00:21:06.710
of the existence of a parser.

00:21:06.710 --> 00:21:10.220
And it's also far
better grounded

00:21:10.220 --> 00:21:12.720
assumption, far better
grounded empirically,

00:21:12.720 --> 00:21:15.540
and with much richer
theoretical structure.

00:21:15.540 --> 00:21:19.350
This is contrary to common
belief, which usually considers

00:21:19.350 --> 00:21:21.990
the two the other way around.

00:21:21.990 --> 00:21:24.360
Nevertheless, let's assume
that a parser exists,

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:26.100
though it isn't obvious.

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:29.190
It's further assumed
that the parser doesn't

00:21:29.190 --> 00:21:33.510
grow the way a language
grows, from some initial state

00:21:33.510 --> 00:21:35.310
to a stable state.

00:21:35.310 --> 00:21:38.460
Now, that assumption, again,
is not very well founded.

00:21:38.460 --> 00:21:40.840
It's based mainly on ignorance.

00:21:40.840 --> 00:21:43.230
It's the simplest assumption,
and we have no reason

00:21:43.230 --> 00:21:46.560
to believe anything about it, so
we might as well believe that.

00:21:46.560 --> 00:21:49.110
There is some kind
of evidence for it,

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:50.860
indirect perhaps,
but interesting.

00:21:50.860 --> 00:21:54.390
There is a forthcoming book
coming out from MIT Press,

00:21:54.390 --> 00:21:59.330
by Robert Berwick and Sandiway
Fong, a former student of his,

00:21:59.330 --> 00:22:02.610
on what you might call a
universal parser, a fixed,

00:22:02.610 --> 00:22:06.780
invariant parser, which has
the property that if you

00:22:06.780 --> 00:22:10.680
flip its switches one way,
it interprets English.

00:22:10.680 --> 00:22:12.990
And if you flip its
switches another way,

00:22:12.990 --> 00:22:15.270
it interprets Japanese.

00:22:15.270 --> 00:22:18.450
And if that's the
right idea, then there

00:22:18.450 --> 00:22:22.200
is a universal parser, which
is unaffected by environmental

00:22:22.200 --> 00:22:25.980
factors, apart from
the properties that

00:22:25.980 --> 00:22:30.120
serve to identify one or
another language, that is,

00:22:30.120 --> 00:22:33.660
the array of
flipping of switches.

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:35.850
I'll come back to that.

00:22:35.850 --> 00:22:36.954
There's a further comment.

00:22:36.954 --> 00:22:38.370
So that assumption,
the assumption

00:22:38.370 --> 00:22:42.330
that the parser doesn't grow,
while strange, could be true.

00:22:42.330 --> 00:22:45.860
And there's only this kind
of indirect evidence for it.

00:22:45.860 --> 00:22:49.660
A further common assumption
is that parsing is easy,

00:22:49.660 --> 00:22:52.680
something that you do
very quickly and easily.

00:22:52.680 --> 00:22:55.320
In fact, that's often
held to be a criterion

00:22:55.320 --> 00:22:56.970
for the theory of language.

00:22:56.970 --> 00:22:59.250
It's often held to provide--

00:22:59.250 --> 00:23:01.710
the criterion is supposed to
be that the theory of language

00:23:01.710 --> 00:23:04.950
must provide generative
procedures that

00:23:04.950 --> 00:23:08.700
satisfy this requirement, that
parsing is easy and quick.

00:23:08.700 --> 00:23:13.440
Well, that one is flatly
false, uncontroversially.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:17.610
Parsing often fails completely
and quite systematically,

00:23:17.610 --> 00:23:19.900
and it's often
extremely difficult.

00:23:19.900 --> 00:23:22.890
Many categories of what
are called parsing failures

00:23:22.890 --> 00:23:25.800
are known, even with
short, simple expressions.

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:28.890
A parsing failure just means
a case where the parser works

00:23:28.890 --> 00:23:31.740
fine, but it gives some kind
of an interpretation which

00:23:31.740 --> 00:23:37.170
is not the one that's assigned
to the signal by the language.

00:23:37.170 --> 00:23:38.520
So it's wrong in that sense.

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:46.020
It's perfectly true that when--
and very often, you can easily

00:23:46.020 --> 00:23:50.060
find categories of expressions,
even pretty simple ones,

00:23:50.060 --> 00:23:51.810
where the parsing
system just breaks down.

00:23:51.810 --> 00:23:53.040
People don't know
what they're hearing.

00:23:53.040 --> 00:23:54.789
It sounds like gibberish,
even though it's

00:23:54.789 --> 00:23:57.570
perfectly well formed and
has fixed, strict meaning,

00:23:57.570 --> 00:23:59.646
so on and so forth.

00:23:59.646 --> 00:24:01.020
Now, it's perfectly
true that one

00:24:01.020 --> 00:24:04.860
uses expressions that
are readily parsed,

00:24:04.860 --> 00:24:07.530
but that verges on tautology.

00:24:07.530 --> 00:24:10.620
What it means is we
use what is usable.

00:24:10.620 --> 00:24:13.950
Other resources of the
language, we just don't use.

00:24:13.950 --> 00:24:15.680
There's a related assumption.

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:18.510
It's often alleged
that language is well

00:24:18.510 --> 00:24:21.910
adapted to the function
of communication.

00:24:21.910 --> 00:24:25.500
It's not clear that that
statement is even meaningful,

00:24:25.500 --> 00:24:28.620
or any similar statements
about biological organs.

00:24:28.620 --> 00:24:30.240
But to the extent
that one can give

00:24:30.240 --> 00:24:33.210
some meaning to that
statement, this one,

00:24:33.210 --> 00:24:35.580
again, looks just false.

00:24:35.580 --> 00:24:39.780
The design of language appears
to make it largely unusable,

00:24:39.780 --> 00:24:40.660
which is fine.

00:24:40.660 --> 00:24:43.200
We just use those fragments
that are usable for us.

00:24:43.200 --> 00:24:47.940
It doesn't mean that the
design made it usable somehow.

00:24:47.940 --> 00:24:52.190
There are similar assumptions
in learnability theory,

00:24:52.190 --> 00:24:53.460
formal learnability theory.

00:24:53.460 --> 00:24:56.100
It's often claimed,
as a starting point,

00:24:56.100 --> 00:24:58.620
that natural languages
must be learnable.

00:24:58.620 --> 00:25:01.290
In fact, natural language
is often defined.

00:25:01.290 --> 00:25:02.940
Natural languages
are defined often

00:25:02.940 --> 00:25:05.010
as the set of languages
that are learnable

00:25:05.010 --> 00:25:07.000
under standard conditions.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:08.770
Now, notice that
that could be true.

00:25:08.770 --> 00:25:10.530
We don't know that
that's false, as we

00:25:10.530 --> 00:25:12.600
know that the comparable
statement about parsing

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:13.540
is false.

00:25:13.540 --> 00:25:16.680
It could be true that
languages are learnable,

00:25:16.680 --> 00:25:19.320
but it's not a
conceptual necessity.

00:25:19.320 --> 00:25:22.480
Language design might be such.

00:25:22.480 --> 00:25:25.190
That's our language
faculty could be such,

00:25:25.190 --> 00:25:29.810
that it permits all kinds
of inaccessible languages.

00:25:29.810 --> 00:25:32.930
These would be realizable
in the brain, just as,

00:25:32.930 --> 00:25:36.050
say, English is, but not
accessible to humans.

00:25:36.050 --> 00:25:39.320
Again, that wouldn't stop
us from doing what we do.

00:25:39.320 --> 00:25:42.240
We would just pick up
the accessible ones.

00:25:42.240 --> 00:25:45.410
In fact, there is some recent
work, quite recent work,

00:25:45.410 --> 00:25:48.470
which suggests that natural
languages are learnable.

00:25:48.470 --> 00:25:51.400
But if true, that's an empirical
discovery and, in fact,

00:25:51.400 --> 00:25:53.390
a rather surprising one.

00:25:53.390 --> 00:25:57.580
Well, I've said nothing so far
about production of language.

00:25:57.580 --> 00:25:59.550
There is a reason for that.

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:02.930
The reason is that apart
from very peripheral aspect,

00:26:02.930 --> 00:26:05.420
it's almost a complete mystery.

00:26:05.420 --> 00:26:08.720
We can learn a good deal
about the mechanisms that

00:26:08.720 --> 00:26:11.180
are put to use when
we speak, and we

00:26:11.180 --> 00:26:14.000
can study at least certain
aspects of perception,

00:26:14.000 --> 00:26:17.660
the mapping of
signals to percept,

00:26:17.660 --> 00:26:20.900
using the internalized
mechanisms,

00:26:20.900 --> 00:26:23.060
the generative procedure
of the language.

00:26:23.060 --> 00:26:27.710
But no one has anything sensible
to say about what I'm doing now

00:26:27.710 --> 00:26:32.300
or what two people are doing
in a normal conversation.

00:26:32.300 --> 00:26:36.590
It was observed centuries ago
that the normal use of language

00:26:36.590 --> 00:26:39.110
has quite curious properties.

00:26:39.110 --> 00:26:40.730
It is unbounded.

00:26:40.730 --> 00:26:42.590
It's not random.

00:26:42.590 --> 00:26:45.110
It is not determined
by external stimuli,

00:26:45.110 --> 00:26:47.240
and there's no reason
to believe that it's

00:26:47.240 --> 00:26:49.610
determined by internal states.

00:26:49.610 --> 00:26:53.900
It's uncaused, but it's somehow
appropriate to situations.

00:26:53.900 --> 00:26:55.550
It's coherent.

00:26:55.550 --> 00:27:00.500
It evokes in the hearer
thoughts that he or she might

00:27:00.500 --> 00:27:02.570
have expressed the same way.

00:27:02.570 --> 00:27:06.050
That collection of properties--
we can call them the creative

00:27:06.050 --> 00:27:08.090
aspect of language use--

00:27:08.090 --> 00:27:14.150
that set of properties was
regarded by the Cartesians

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:17.000
as the best evidence
that another organism has

00:27:17.000 --> 00:27:21.380
a mind like ours, not
just a body, like a dog

00:27:21.380 --> 00:27:23.495
or a monkey or a robot.

00:27:23.495 --> 00:27:25.370
Actually, if you look
back at their argument,

00:27:25.370 --> 00:27:27.270
it was quite reasonable.

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:29.000
The science happened
to be wrong,

00:27:29.000 --> 00:27:30.290
but it was very reasonable.

00:27:30.290 --> 00:27:32.480
And within the framework
of scientific reasoning,

00:27:32.480 --> 00:27:35.450
we might put matters
in different terms.

00:27:35.450 --> 00:27:39.080
But we haven't made any
progress in understanding

00:27:39.080 --> 00:27:43.070
these critically important
phenomena, the phenomena that

00:27:43.070 --> 00:27:46.495
concerned them and
that rely much--

00:27:46.495 --> 00:27:49.730
the provide much of the basis
for the traditional mind-body

00:27:49.730 --> 00:27:51.140
distinction.

00:27:51.140 --> 00:27:53.330
In my opinion, in fact,
contemporary thought

00:27:53.330 --> 00:27:55.220
about these topics,
usually phrased

00:27:55.220 --> 00:27:58.220
in terms of notions
like the Turing test,

00:27:58.220 --> 00:28:00.740
involves a
considerable regression

00:28:00.740 --> 00:28:02.660
from the much more
scientific approach

00:28:02.660 --> 00:28:04.870
of Cartesian psychology.

00:28:04.870 --> 00:28:08.450
I'm not going to have time
to elaborate properly.

00:28:08.450 --> 00:28:13.700
I merely mention this to provoke
some possible interest or maybe

00:28:13.700 --> 00:28:14.420
outrage.

00:28:14.420 --> 00:28:16.640
We'll see.

00:28:16.640 --> 00:28:19.940
Well, I've mentioned
several kinds

00:28:19.940 --> 00:28:24.890
of shared interest in the early
modern cognitive sciences,

00:28:24.890 --> 00:28:29.180
say, back to the 1956 meeting
that George Miller pointed to.

00:28:29.180 --> 00:28:31.040
One common interest,
again, is in

00:28:31.040 --> 00:28:33.710
computational
representational systems,

00:28:33.710 --> 00:28:37.400
looking at the brain in
terms of software problems.

00:28:37.400 --> 00:28:41.240
For linguistics, at
least, this turned

00:28:41.240 --> 00:28:43.430
into the study of the
generative procedures that

00:28:43.430 --> 00:28:46.580
are put to use in
perception and presumably,

00:28:46.580 --> 00:28:48.530
an expression of
thought speaking,

00:28:48.530 --> 00:28:52.640
although the latter again
remains a complete mystery.

00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:54.400
There's another shared interest.

00:28:54.400 --> 00:28:56.870
It's what we might call
the unification problem.

00:28:56.870 --> 00:29:00.260
Now, that's a problem that
has two different aspects.

00:29:00.260 --> 00:29:03.830
One aspect is the relation
between the hardware

00:29:03.830 --> 00:29:06.200
and the software,
the relationship

00:29:06.200 --> 00:29:08.810
between computational
representational theories

00:29:08.810 --> 00:29:13.550
of the brain and the
entities that they postulate,

00:29:13.550 --> 00:29:16.770
and what you might
call implementation.

00:29:16.770 --> 00:29:18.440
And as I suggested,
I think you can

00:29:18.440 --> 00:29:21.770
regard that as similar to
the problem of the relation

00:29:21.770 --> 00:29:24.260
between, say, chemistry
and physics 100 years ago

00:29:24.260 --> 00:29:28.100
or genetics and chemistry
50 years ago, and so on.

00:29:28.100 --> 00:29:30.020
That's one problem,
the unification

00:29:30.020 --> 00:29:32.570
generally, one aspect
of the unification

00:29:32.570 --> 00:29:34.590
problem, the deepest aspect.

00:29:34.590 --> 00:29:38.600
A less deep aspect has
to do with the relation

00:29:38.600 --> 00:29:41.840
among the various domains
of cognitive science, say,

00:29:41.840 --> 00:29:44.600
language and
vision, whatever may

00:29:44.600 --> 00:29:46.800
be involved in problem solving.

00:29:46.800 --> 00:29:48.980
This is the question, what
do they have in common?

00:29:48.980 --> 00:29:52.340
Notice that that's much
less profound a problem,

00:29:52.340 --> 00:29:55.100
if it turns out the answer is
nothing, just the way it is.

00:29:57.815 --> 00:30:00.440
On the other hand, we'd be upset
if the answer to the first one

00:30:00.440 --> 00:30:02.940
was no answer.

00:30:02.940 --> 00:30:07.414
Well, at this point, some of my
skepticism begins to surface.

00:30:07.414 --> 00:30:09.330
So let's take the first
one, the deep problem,

00:30:09.330 --> 00:30:11.570
the serious,
scientific problem--

00:30:11.570 --> 00:30:16.220
the relation between roughly
the hardware and software,

00:30:16.220 --> 00:30:17.890
chemistry and physics.

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:22.920
Here, there are two different
approaches you can identify.

00:30:22.920 --> 00:30:26.700
One approach== let's
call it naturalistic--

00:30:26.700 --> 00:30:28.480
says, it goes like this.

00:30:28.480 --> 00:30:30.510
It says, the mental
organs are viewed

00:30:30.510 --> 00:30:32.700
as computational
representational systems,

00:30:32.700 --> 00:30:34.330
and we want to show
that they're real,

00:30:34.330 --> 00:30:37.380
that their statements in
these theories are true,

00:30:37.380 --> 00:30:40.320
that is, that there are certain
states and the properties

00:30:40.320 --> 00:30:46.530
of the brain which these
theories exactly capture,

00:30:46.530 --> 00:30:49.080
in that's what you try to show.

00:30:49.080 --> 00:30:51.996
The mental organs can also
be viewed in other ways.

00:30:51.996 --> 00:30:53.370
For example, you
can look at them

00:30:53.370 --> 00:30:56.334
in terms of cells or atoms.

00:30:56.334 --> 00:30:59.070
Or more interestingly,
for the moment,

00:30:59.070 --> 00:31:01.840
you can look at them in
terms of electrical activity

00:31:01.840 --> 00:31:06.394
of the brain, so-called event
related potentials, ERPs.

00:31:06.394 --> 00:31:08.610
Now, rather
surprisingly, there's

00:31:08.610 --> 00:31:12.810
some recent work which shows
quite dramatic and surprising

00:31:12.810 --> 00:31:15.570
correlations between
certain properties

00:31:15.570 --> 00:31:17.640
of the computational
representational systems

00:31:17.640 --> 00:31:24.000
of language and of
ERPs, evoked potentials.

00:31:24.000 --> 00:31:28.140
For the moment,
these ERP measures

00:31:28.140 --> 00:31:32.760
have no status apart from their
correlation with categories

00:31:32.760 --> 00:31:36.270
of expressions that come out of
computational representational

00:31:36.270 --> 00:31:37.000
theories.

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:40.450
That is, they're just
numbers picked at random,

00:31:40.450 --> 00:31:42.840
because there's no relevant
theory about them that

00:31:42.840 --> 00:31:45.600
says, just look at these numbers
and not at some other numbers.

00:31:45.600 --> 00:31:48.360
In themselves, in other
words, they're curiosities.

00:31:48.360 --> 00:31:51.180
Still, it's interesting,
because they do--

00:31:51.180 --> 00:31:55.080
or there are correlations to
rather subtle properties that

00:31:55.080 --> 00:31:57.660
have emerged in the
attempt to develop

00:31:57.660 --> 00:32:00.510
computational representational
theories which explain the form

00:32:00.510 --> 00:32:02.970
and meaning of language.

00:32:02.970 --> 00:32:06.320
So that suggests an interesting
direction for research,

00:32:06.320 --> 00:32:09.660
namely to try to unify these
quite different approaches

00:32:09.660 --> 00:32:12.480
to the brain, and to
place each of them

00:32:12.480 --> 00:32:14.550
in an appropriate
theoretical context.

00:32:14.550 --> 00:32:18.480
For the moment, that's primarily
a problem for the ERPs,

00:32:18.480 --> 00:32:20.389
but if you can pursue
it, it could be

00:32:20.389 --> 00:32:21.680
quite an interesting direction.

00:32:21.680 --> 00:32:24.730
And one can think of
lots of consequences.

00:32:24.730 --> 00:32:28.260
Well, the same is true of the
relation between CR systems,

00:32:28.260 --> 00:32:30.060
computational
representational systems,

00:32:30.060 --> 00:32:33.180
and physiology, at
least in principle.

00:32:33.180 --> 00:32:36.450
Here, it's just in principle,
because in practice at present,

00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:39.000
we don't really
have much to look

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:40.890
at there except
from the theories

00:32:40.890 --> 00:32:43.970
of the computational
representational systems.

00:32:43.970 --> 00:32:46.980
But here, whatever the
difficulties may be,

00:32:46.980 --> 00:32:50.690
we seem to face ordinary
problems of science before

00:32:50.690 --> 00:32:55.230
the typical situation when
alternative approaches

00:32:55.230 --> 00:32:58.590
don't-- there's no understood
way to unify them or connect

00:32:58.590 --> 00:33:00.030
them.

00:33:00.030 --> 00:33:01.620
Well, there's a
different approach

00:33:01.620 --> 00:33:03.270
to this unification
problem, which

00:33:03.270 --> 00:33:05.940
actually dominates
contemporary thinking

00:33:05.940 --> 00:33:07.320
about cognitive science--

00:33:07.320 --> 00:33:09.630
not necessarily the actual
work, but the thinking

00:33:09.630 --> 00:33:11.370
about what it amounts to.

00:33:11.370 --> 00:33:14.640
This approach is to divorce
the study of mental organs

00:33:14.640 --> 00:33:17.130
from the biological
setting altogether

00:33:17.130 --> 00:33:20.610
and to ask whether some
simulation can, essentially,

00:33:20.610 --> 00:33:22.980
fool an outside observer.

00:33:22.980 --> 00:33:27.930
So we ask whether the simulation
passes some rather arbitrary

00:33:27.930 --> 00:33:30.030
test, called the Turing test.

00:33:30.030 --> 00:33:33.000
But Turing test is just
a name for a huge battery

00:33:33.000 --> 00:33:35.040
of possible tests.

00:33:35.040 --> 00:33:38.810
And so we pick out one
of those at random,

00:33:38.810 --> 00:33:41.820
and we now ask
whether some, say,

00:33:41.820 --> 00:33:44.130
extraterrestrial
invented organism

00:33:44.130 --> 00:33:46.950
or some actual
programmed computer

00:33:46.950 --> 00:33:50.280
passes the test that
has fooled somebody.

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:52.200
And people write
articles in which

00:33:52.200 --> 00:33:55.920
they ask, how can we
determine empirically

00:33:55.920 --> 00:34:00.810
whether an extraterrestrial or
a programmed computer or machine

00:34:00.810 --> 00:34:04.505
can, say, play chess
or understand Chinese?

00:34:04.505 --> 00:34:05.880
And the answer is
supposed to be,

00:34:05.880 --> 00:34:09.270
if it can fool an observer
under some arbitrary

00:34:09.270 --> 00:34:11.130
conditions, called
the Turing test,

00:34:11.130 --> 00:34:13.800
then we say we've
empirically established that.

00:34:13.800 --> 00:34:17.489
Actually, you may have
noticed, last November, there

00:34:17.489 --> 00:34:23.699
was a big splash about a
running of the Turing test,

00:34:23.699 --> 00:34:26.730
over at the Boston
Computer Museum,

00:34:26.730 --> 00:34:29.190
together with the Cambridge
Center for Behavioral Studies

00:34:29.190 --> 00:34:31.590
and a quite distinguished
panel, that a bunch

00:34:31.590 --> 00:34:34.550
of programs which were
supposed to try to fool people.

00:34:34.550 --> 00:34:37.710
There was, I think, a
$100,000 prize offered,

00:34:37.710 --> 00:34:41.520
a big story in the Boston Globe,
front page story in the New

00:34:41.520 --> 00:34:42.179
York Times.

00:34:42.179 --> 00:34:45.659
Science had a big splash on it.

00:34:45.659 --> 00:34:48.570
I wasn't there, but I'm told
that the program that did best

00:34:48.570 --> 00:34:51.780
in fooling people into thinking
that it was a human was one

00:34:51.780 --> 00:34:53.880
that produced clichés at random.

00:34:53.880 --> 00:34:55.170
[LAUGHTER]

00:34:55.170 --> 00:34:56.785
Apparently, it did pretty well.

00:34:56.785 --> 00:34:57.740
I don't know.

00:34:57.740 --> 00:35:02.130
It may tell you something about
humans, but I'm not sure what.

00:35:02.130 --> 00:35:07.470
As far as I can see, all of
this is entirely pointless.

00:35:07.470 --> 00:35:11.370
It's like asking how we
could determine empirically

00:35:11.370 --> 00:35:14.250
whether an airplane
can fly, the answer

00:35:14.250 --> 00:35:16.560
being if it can fool
someone into thinking

00:35:16.560 --> 00:35:19.500
that it's an eagle, say,
under some conditions.

00:35:19.500 --> 00:35:23.620
But the question whether
an airplane can fly

00:35:23.620 --> 00:35:25.920
and whether it's really
flying or for that matter,

00:35:25.920 --> 00:35:28.710
the question whether
high jumpers were really

00:35:28.710 --> 00:35:31.020
flying at the last
Olympics, that's

00:35:31.020 --> 00:35:32.850
not an empirical question.

00:35:32.850 --> 00:35:35.820
That's a matter of decision,
and different languages

00:35:35.820 --> 00:35:38.170
make decisions differently.

00:35:38.170 --> 00:35:44.070
So if you're speaking Japanese,
I'm told airplanes, eagles,

00:35:44.070 --> 00:35:47.580
and high jumpers all
are really flying.

00:35:47.580 --> 00:35:51.300
If you're talking English,
airplanes and eagles

00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:55.530
are really flying, but humans
aren't, except metaphorically.

00:35:55.530 --> 00:35:59.040
If you're talking Hebrew,
eagles are flying,

00:35:59.040 --> 00:36:02.770
and neither airplanes
nor humans are.

00:36:02.770 --> 00:36:05.385
But that's just a matter of--

00:36:05.385 --> 00:36:07.380
there's no substantive
issue involved.

00:36:07.380 --> 00:36:09.860
There's nothing to
this to determine,

00:36:09.860 --> 00:36:12.060
no question to answer.

00:36:12.060 --> 00:36:15.600
Simulation of an eagle could
have some purposes, of course,

00:36:15.600 --> 00:36:17.940
say, learning about
eagles or maybe solving

00:36:17.940 --> 00:36:20.190
some problem in aerodynamics.

00:36:20.190 --> 00:36:24.150
But fooling an observer under
some variety of the Turing test

00:36:24.150 --> 00:36:27.150
is not a sensible purpose,
as far as I can see.

00:36:27.150 --> 00:36:28.650
As far as I'm aware,
there's nothing

00:36:28.650 --> 00:36:30.660
like this in the sciences.

00:36:30.660 --> 00:36:33.960
Incidentally, if you go back
to the 17th and 18th centuries,

00:36:33.960 --> 00:36:35.790
scientists were very much in--

00:36:35.790 --> 00:36:39.060
who we call philosophers,
but they didn't distinguish--

00:36:39.060 --> 00:36:43.770
they were greatly intrigued
by automata, by machines,

00:36:43.770 --> 00:36:45.690
for example, automata
that simulated,

00:36:45.690 --> 00:36:48.150
say, the digestion of a duck.

00:36:48.150 --> 00:36:51.630
But their goal was
to learn about ducks,

00:36:51.630 --> 00:36:53.880
not to answer
meaningless questions

00:36:53.880 --> 00:36:56.880
about whether machines
can really digest,

00:36:56.880 --> 00:37:00.570
as proven by the fact that
they can fool an observer who's

00:37:00.570 --> 00:37:03.190
just, say, looking behind
a screen or something,

00:37:03.190 --> 00:37:05.780
however they may be doing it.

00:37:05.780 --> 00:37:07.626
Well, this is one
of the respects

00:37:07.626 --> 00:37:09.000
in which, in my
opinion at least,

00:37:09.000 --> 00:37:13.530
there's been regression since
the 17th and 18th century.

00:37:13.530 --> 00:37:16.770
Chess playing programs are
a perfect example of this,

00:37:16.770 --> 00:37:18.420
in my opinion.

00:37:18.420 --> 00:37:20.640
Well, anyway, that's
two approaches

00:37:20.640 --> 00:37:22.560
to the first question
of unification,

00:37:22.560 --> 00:37:24.440
the sort of deep one.

00:37:24.440 --> 00:37:26.280
What about the more
shallow question

00:37:26.280 --> 00:37:28.740
of unification, that is,
the question of unification

00:37:28.740 --> 00:37:32.160
among the various branches
of the cognitive sciences?

00:37:32.160 --> 00:37:35.550
Is there any real
commonality among them?

00:37:35.550 --> 00:37:38.280
Well, that doesn't
seem entirely obvious.

00:37:38.280 --> 00:37:43.170
So take what you might think
of, what you might call,

00:37:43.170 --> 00:37:46.620
physical organs below the
neck, metaphorically speaking,

00:37:46.620 --> 00:37:49.580
meaning not the
ones we call mental.

00:37:49.580 --> 00:37:53.510
Say, take the circulatory
system and the digestive system,

00:37:53.510 --> 00:37:55.620
the visual system, and so on.

00:37:55.620 --> 00:37:59.880
These are organs of the body in
some reasonable sense, viewed

00:37:59.880 --> 00:38:03.810
abstractly, and
that's a normal way

00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:06.180
to study any complex system.

00:38:06.180 --> 00:38:08.040
The study of these
abstract organs

00:38:08.040 --> 00:38:09.870
does fall together
at some level,

00:38:09.870 --> 00:38:12.979
we assume, presumably at the
level of cellular biology.

00:38:12.979 --> 00:38:15.270
But it's not at all clear
that there's any other level,

00:38:15.270 --> 00:38:17.880
that is, that there is
some kind of organ theory

00:38:17.880 --> 00:38:20.040
at which these
systems are unified.

00:38:20.040 --> 00:38:22.020
Maybe, but it's not obvious.

00:38:22.020 --> 00:38:25.100
Well, the brain is
scarcely understood,

00:38:25.100 --> 00:38:28.170
but to the extent that
anything's understood about it,

00:38:28.170 --> 00:38:30.840
it appears to be an
extremely intricate system,

00:38:30.840 --> 00:38:33.180
heterogeneous, with all
kind of subparts that

00:38:33.180 --> 00:38:35.730
have special design, and so on.

00:38:35.730 --> 00:38:37.890
And there's no special
reason to suppose

00:38:37.890 --> 00:38:39.990
that the mental
organs are unified,

00:38:39.990 --> 00:38:42.480
in some kind of
mental organ theory,

00:38:42.480 --> 00:38:44.377
above the level of
cellular biology,

00:38:44.377 --> 00:38:46.710
that is, above the level at
which they may fall together

00:38:46.710 --> 00:38:49.830
with the circulatory
system, and so on.

00:38:49.830 --> 00:38:51.570
If there is no
intermediate level,

00:38:51.570 --> 00:38:54.810
no such unification, then
there is no cognitive science.

00:38:54.810 --> 00:38:57.930
Hence, there was no
cognitive revolution

00:38:57.930 --> 00:39:01.230
except for similarities of
perspective, which could

00:39:01.230 --> 00:39:03.690
be quite interesting, in fact.

00:39:03.690 --> 00:39:05.317
Well, there are
various speculations

00:39:05.317 --> 00:39:06.150
about this question.

00:39:06.150 --> 00:39:10.860
And in fact, they go right
back to that 1956 meeting.

00:39:10.860 --> 00:39:13.530
One general line
of speculation is

00:39:13.530 --> 00:39:16.740
that the mind is what's
called modular, that is,

00:39:16.740 --> 00:39:19.020
that mental organs
have special design,

00:39:19.020 --> 00:39:21.900
like everything else we
know in the physical world.

00:39:21.900 --> 00:39:27.480
The other approach assumes
uniformity, that there are,

00:39:27.480 --> 00:39:29.340
what are called,
general mechanisms

00:39:29.340 --> 00:39:31.380
of learning or
thinking, which are just

00:39:31.380 --> 00:39:33.060
applied to different domains.

00:39:33.060 --> 00:39:36.730
They're applied to language
or to chess, and so on.

00:39:36.730 --> 00:39:40.140
And as I say, these differences
didn't exactly surface,

00:39:40.140 --> 00:39:43.620
but they were more or less
implicit at the 1956 meeting.

00:39:43.620 --> 00:39:48.090
The paper on generative grammar
took for granted modularity,

00:39:48.090 --> 00:39:54.430
that is, a rich, specific
genetic endowment, the language

00:39:54.430 --> 00:39:59.650
faculty's special system, and
no general mental organ theory.

00:39:59.650 --> 00:40:03.610
The Newell-Simon approach, the
study of the logic machine,

00:40:03.610 --> 00:40:06.820
that took for granted the
opposite position, that is,

00:40:06.820 --> 00:40:09.800
that there are general
mechanisms of problem solving

00:40:09.800 --> 00:40:14.290
which are quite indifferent
as to subject matter--

00:40:14.290 --> 00:40:17.020
in our case, theorem proving,
but equivalently, chess

00:40:17.020 --> 00:40:18.820
or language, or whatever.

00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:20.800
And you can trace these
different intuitions

00:40:20.800 --> 00:40:24.560
back quite a way, quite
back 100s of years, in fact.

00:40:24.560 --> 00:40:27.040
They're very lively
today in the discussions

00:40:27.040 --> 00:40:29.680
about connectionist
work, for example.

00:40:29.680 --> 00:40:32.710
My own view is that the
evidence is overwhelmingly

00:40:32.710 --> 00:40:35.720
in favor of the highly
modular approaches.

00:40:35.720 --> 00:40:38.890
There are only a few areas
where we have any real insight,

00:40:38.890 --> 00:40:42.970
and these invariably seem to
involve quite special design.

00:40:42.970 --> 00:40:45.880
Possibly, there are some
unifying principles, but about

00:40:45.880 --> 00:40:49.660
these, if they exist,
nothing much is understood.

00:40:49.660 --> 00:40:53.220
Again, that's a minority
view, but I can only tell you

00:40:53.220 --> 00:40:55.390
the way it looks to me.

00:40:55.390 --> 00:40:57.640
Even internal to the
language faculty,

00:40:57.640 --> 00:41:00.850
there seems to be quite
highly modular design.

00:41:00.850 --> 00:41:03.400
So let's distinguish
roughly between what

00:41:03.400 --> 00:41:06.670
we might call grammatical
ability, meaning the ability

00:41:06.670 --> 00:41:09.010
to produce properly
formed expressions,

00:41:09.010 --> 00:41:12.610
and conceptual ability, that
is, to produce expressions that

00:41:12.610 --> 00:41:16.270
somehow make some sense,
and pragmatic ability, that

00:41:16.270 --> 00:41:18.580
is, the ability
to use expressions

00:41:18.580 --> 00:41:21.040
in some way appropriate
to the occasion.

00:41:21.040 --> 00:41:26.950
Well, these capacities have been
discovered to be dissociated

00:41:26.950 --> 00:41:30.360
developmentally-- like one can
develop and the other is not--

00:41:30.360 --> 00:41:33.850
and selectively
impaired under injury.

00:41:33.850 --> 00:41:36.580
And their properties
are quite different.

00:41:36.580 --> 00:41:39.160
And they seem to be radically
different from what we find

00:41:39.160 --> 00:41:40.990
in other cognitive systems.

00:41:40.990 --> 00:41:45.220
So even internally, just
at the most gross look,

00:41:45.220 --> 00:41:47.300
we seem to find modularity.

00:41:47.300 --> 00:41:49.900
And when you look more closely
into subsystems of language,

00:41:49.900 --> 00:41:52.120
they just seem to behave
rather differently.

00:41:52.120 --> 00:41:54.280
I don't see why this
should surprise anyone.

00:41:54.280 --> 00:41:57.036
It's exactly what we find
about every other biological

00:41:57.036 --> 00:41:59.410
organism, so why shouldn't it
be true of the brain, which

00:41:59.410 --> 00:42:01.900
is maybe the most
complex one around?

00:42:01.900 --> 00:42:04.630
But it's considered a very
controversial position.

00:42:04.630 --> 00:42:06.400
Again, I stress,
as far as I know,

00:42:06.400 --> 00:42:09.730
all the evidence supports it,
and it shouldn't be surprising.

00:42:09.730 --> 00:42:13.699
Well, just how modular
is the language faculty?

00:42:13.699 --> 00:42:14.990
That's an interesting question.

00:42:14.990 --> 00:42:17.410
It doesn't seem so
special that it's

00:42:17.410 --> 00:42:20.020
linked to particular
processing systems.

00:42:20.020 --> 00:42:25.150
Here, a study of sign language
in the last decade or so,

00:42:25.150 --> 00:42:27.820
maybe 20 years, has
been quite interesting.

00:42:27.820 --> 00:42:32.200
Rather surprisingly, it
seems that sign language,

00:42:32.200 --> 00:42:34.930
the normal language of
the deaf, sign language

00:42:34.930 --> 00:42:37.630
is localized in speech
areas of the brain,

00:42:37.630 --> 00:42:40.240
in the left hemisphere and
not in the visual processing

00:42:40.240 --> 00:42:42.780
areas-- which you might
expect, since you see it.

00:42:42.780 --> 00:42:44.090
You don't hear it.

00:42:44.090 --> 00:42:47.250
That's shown pretty
well by aphasia studies

00:42:47.250 --> 00:42:50.020
and again, ERP studies.

00:42:50.020 --> 00:42:51.940
Also, it turns out
that children who

00:42:51.940 --> 00:42:54.910
are acquiring sign language
in a natural setting

00:42:54.910 --> 00:42:57.400
go through steps-- at least
in the early stages, which

00:42:57.400 --> 00:42:59.110
have been studied--
that are remarkably

00:42:59.110 --> 00:43:02.500
similar to the acquisition
of spoken language and timing

00:43:02.500 --> 00:43:03.820
and everything else.

00:43:03.820 --> 00:43:07.690
They even override
obvious iconic properties

00:43:07.690 --> 00:43:09.130
of visual symbols.

00:43:09.130 --> 00:43:11.110
So pointing is
overridden and given

00:43:11.110 --> 00:43:13.600
a symbolic interpretation.

00:43:13.600 --> 00:43:15.610
Furthermore, acquisition
of sign language

00:43:15.610 --> 00:43:17.800
turns out to be quite
different from acquisition

00:43:17.800 --> 00:43:20.470
of communicative gestures.

00:43:20.470 --> 00:43:23.620
There's even one case on
record of spontaneous invention

00:43:23.620 --> 00:43:26.380
of sign language by
three deaf children,

00:43:26.380 --> 00:43:29.230
who had no environmental
model and no stimulus

00:43:29.230 --> 00:43:31.705
at all, as far as anyone
can be determined.

00:43:31.705 --> 00:43:34.390
And that's the
perfect experiment.

00:43:34.390 --> 00:43:37.690
The system that these
three kids constructed

00:43:37.690 --> 00:43:41.080
has very standard properties
of natural language, natural

00:43:41.080 --> 00:43:43.870
spoken language, and the
developmental stages also

00:43:43.870 --> 00:43:45.610
appear to be similar.

00:43:45.610 --> 00:43:48.640
That looks like about a pure
expression of the language

00:43:48.640 --> 00:43:52.960
faculty unaffected
by experience,

00:43:52.960 --> 00:43:56.710
and it suggests that however
modular the system may be,

00:43:56.710 --> 00:44:00.010
it's not so modular that it's
keyed to a particular mode

00:44:00.010 --> 00:44:03.790
of articulation or input.

00:44:03.790 --> 00:44:06.580
What's now known suggests
that the language faculty

00:44:06.580 --> 00:44:11.310
simply grows a language, much as
other organs of the body grow.

00:44:11.310 --> 00:44:14.430
And it does so in a way largely
determined by its inner nature,

00:44:14.430 --> 00:44:16.650
by properties of
the initial state.

00:44:16.650 --> 00:44:18.940
External events doubtless
have some kind of an effect,

00:44:18.940 --> 00:44:21.960
like I'm talking
English, not Japanese.

00:44:21.960 --> 00:44:25.890
But a rational Martian
scientist who is looking at us

00:44:25.890 --> 00:44:29.830
would probably not find these
variations very impressive.

00:44:29.830 --> 00:44:33.030
And the language that
grows in the mind

00:44:33.030 --> 00:44:35.220
appears to be, at
least partially,

00:44:35.220 --> 00:44:37.650
independent of sensory modality.

00:44:37.650 --> 00:44:40.140
Well, at this point, we're
moving toward serious inquiry

00:44:40.140 --> 00:44:44.930
into modular architecture
of mind and brain,

00:44:44.930 --> 00:44:47.520
in some of the few areas where
it's possible to undertake it,

00:44:47.520 --> 00:44:49.860
and with some
surprising results.

00:44:49.860 --> 00:44:52.440
Well, one last
element of skepticism

00:44:52.440 --> 00:44:55.500
about the cognitive
revolution has

00:44:55.500 --> 00:44:58.620
to do with just how much
of a revolution it was.

00:44:58.620 --> 00:45:02.150
In fact, to a far greater extent
than was realized at that time,

00:45:02.150 --> 00:45:05.280
or is even generally
recognized today,

00:45:05.280 --> 00:45:08.760
the shift in
perspective in the 1950s

00:45:08.760 --> 00:45:13.500
recapitulated and rediscovered
some long-forgotten ideas

00:45:13.500 --> 00:45:16.320
and insights from what you
might call the first cognitive

00:45:16.320 --> 00:45:19.230
revolution, which
pretty much coincided

00:45:19.230 --> 00:45:23.340
with the scientific revolution
of the 17th and 18th centuries.

00:45:23.340 --> 00:45:26.640
Now, the Galilean-Newtonian
revolution in physics

00:45:26.640 --> 00:45:28.020
is well known.

00:45:28.020 --> 00:45:31.300
The Cartesian revolution
and its aftermath

00:45:31.300 --> 00:45:34.570
in psychology and physiology
is not very well known.

00:45:34.570 --> 00:45:40.340
But it was quite dramatic,
and it had important effects.

00:45:40.340 --> 00:45:42.420
It had important consequences.

00:45:42.420 --> 00:45:46.680
It also developed some of the
major themes that were taken up

00:45:46.680 --> 00:45:50.940
again since the 1950s, primarily
in the areas of language

00:45:50.940 --> 00:45:53.070
and vision, which
are perhaps the most

00:45:53.070 --> 00:45:56.040
successful branches of
contemporary cognitive science,

00:45:56.040 --> 00:45:57.680
as well.

00:45:57.680 --> 00:46:00.870
Now, this first
cognitive revolution,

00:46:00.870 --> 00:46:02.960
though it had a great
deal of interest,

00:46:02.960 --> 00:46:07.755
and there's much you can learn
from it, it did face barriers.

00:46:07.755 --> 00:46:10.472
And in fact, they were
insuperable barriers.

00:46:10.472 --> 00:46:12.180
So for example, in
the study of language,

00:46:12.180 --> 00:46:15.840
it came to be realized soon
enough that human language is

00:46:15.840 --> 00:46:19.560
somehow a process, not
just a dead product.

00:46:19.560 --> 00:46:20.940
It's not a bunch of texts.

00:46:20.940 --> 00:46:22.980
It's some process that goes on.

00:46:22.980 --> 00:46:25.170
Furthermore, it's a
process that makes

00:46:25.170 --> 00:46:29.340
infinite use of finite means,
as Wilhelm von Humboldt put it

00:46:29.340 --> 00:46:31.180
in the early 19th century.

00:46:31.180 --> 00:46:33.960
But no sense could be
made of these ideas,

00:46:33.960 --> 00:46:38.460
and the inquiry aborted,
aborted for well over a century.

00:46:38.460 --> 00:46:41.160
By this century,
the formal sciences

00:46:41.160 --> 00:46:44.370
had provided enough
understanding of all of this,

00:46:44.370 --> 00:46:49.500
so that many traditional themes
could be productively engaged,

00:46:49.500 --> 00:46:52.440
particularly against the
background of other advances

00:46:52.440 --> 00:46:55.840
in science and engineering and
anthropological linguistics.

00:46:55.840 --> 00:46:57.810
And one could
understand quite well

00:46:57.810 --> 00:47:01.300
what it meant to make
infinite use of finite means.

00:47:01.300 --> 00:47:04.050
Well, let's turn to
a quick look at some

00:47:04.050 --> 00:47:06.439
of the kinds of
questions that arise

00:47:06.439 --> 00:47:07.980
and some of the
kinds of answers that

00:47:07.980 --> 00:47:12.420
can be offered with respect
to language specifically.

00:47:12.420 --> 00:47:14.710
So take something
that everybody knows.

00:47:14.710 --> 00:47:17.580
Start with something really
trivial, very simple.

00:47:17.580 --> 00:47:21.240
So take a simple phrase,
like, say, a brown house.

00:47:21.240 --> 00:47:23.790
You and I have
generative procedures

00:47:23.790 --> 00:47:27.210
which are more or less similar,
and these generative procedures

00:47:27.210 --> 00:47:30.040
determine what we know
about this expression.

00:47:30.040 --> 00:47:32.820
For example, we know that
it consists of two words.

00:47:32.820 --> 00:47:35.430
We know that those two
words have the same vowel

00:47:35.430 --> 00:47:37.770
for most speakers.

00:47:37.770 --> 00:47:39.652
We know also that if
something or other,

00:47:39.652 --> 00:47:41.110
if I point to
something, and I say,

00:47:41.110 --> 00:47:46.290
that's a brown house, what I
mean is its exterior is brown.

00:47:46.290 --> 00:47:49.590
If somebody paints
a house brown,

00:47:49.590 --> 00:47:52.800
we know that they painted
the exterior brown.

00:47:52.800 --> 00:47:54.390
If they painted
the inside brown,

00:47:54.390 --> 00:47:56.940
they didn't paint
the house brown.

00:47:56.940 --> 00:48:01.050
If you see a house,
you see its exterior.

00:48:01.050 --> 00:48:03.720
So we can't see this
building, as a matter

00:48:03.720 --> 00:48:04.830
of conceptual necessity.

00:48:04.830 --> 00:48:07.440
If we were standing
outside, we might.

00:48:07.440 --> 00:48:09.360
And the same is
true of a whole mass

00:48:09.360 --> 00:48:12.600
of what are sometimes called
container words, like box

00:48:12.600 --> 00:48:15.490
or igloo or airplane.

00:48:15.490 --> 00:48:19.050
You can see an airplane, for
example, if you're inside it,

00:48:19.050 --> 00:48:21.630
but only if you can look out
the window and see the wing,

00:48:21.630 --> 00:48:23.370
or if there's a
mirror outside which

00:48:23.370 --> 00:48:25.230
reflects the exterior
of the airplane,

00:48:25.230 --> 00:48:26.400
or something like that.

00:48:26.400 --> 00:48:28.890
Then you could see the
airplane, otherwise not.

00:48:28.890 --> 00:48:31.530
The same is true with
invented concepts,

00:48:31.530 --> 00:48:33.090
even impossible concepts.

00:48:33.090 --> 00:48:35.460
So, say, take a spherical cube.

00:48:35.460 --> 00:48:38.340
If somebody paints this
spherical cube brown,

00:48:38.340 --> 00:48:40.890
that means they painted
the exterior brown, not

00:48:40.890 --> 00:48:42.810
the interior.

00:48:42.810 --> 00:48:45.120
So all of these, a house
that's [INAUDIBLE] and all

00:48:45.120 --> 00:48:47.260
these things are
exterior surfaces,

00:48:47.260 --> 00:48:49.230
which is kind of
curious to start with.

00:48:49.230 --> 00:48:51.720
However, they're
not just exteriors.

00:48:51.720 --> 00:48:54.930
So suppose two people
say John and Mary are

00:48:54.930 --> 00:48:57.920
equidistant from the
exterior regarded

00:48:57.920 --> 00:48:59.080
as a mathematical object.

00:48:59.080 --> 00:49:01.580
Suppose they're equidistant
from the exterior,

00:49:01.580 --> 00:49:06.100
but John is outside it,
and Mary's inside it.

00:49:06.100 --> 00:49:10.480
We can ask whether John, the guy
outside, is nearer the house,

00:49:10.480 --> 00:49:12.250
or whatever it is,
and there's an answer,

00:49:12.250 --> 00:49:14.980
depending on current
criteria for nearness.

00:49:14.980 --> 00:49:16.870
But we can't ask it about Mary.

00:49:16.870 --> 00:49:20.830
We're not near this building,
no matter what the criteria are.

00:49:20.830 --> 00:49:24.250
So it's not just an exterior.

00:49:24.250 --> 00:49:28.810
It's an exterior plus something
about a distinguished interior.

00:49:28.810 --> 00:49:30.310
However, the nature
of that interior

00:49:30.310 --> 00:49:32.050
doesn't seem to
matter very much.

00:49:32.050 --> 00:49:35.080
So it's the same house if
you, say, take it and fill it

00:49:35.080 --> 00:49:37.120
with cheese, or
something like that.

00:49:37.120 --> 00:49:39.040
You change the exterior,
it's the same house.

00:49:39.040 --> 00:49:41.795
It hasn't changed at all.

00:49:41.795 --> 00:49:43.830
And similarly, you can
move the walls around,

00:49:43.830 --> 00:49:45.730
and it stays the same house.

00:49:45.730 --> 00:49:47.950
On the other hand,
you can clean a house

00:49:47.950 --> 00:49:49.720
and not touch the
exterior at all.

00:49:49.720 --> 00:49:52.330
You can only do things
to the interior.

00:49:52.330 --> 00:49:55.870
So somehow, it's a very
special combination

00:49:55.870 --> 00:49:59.320
of a abstract, though
somehow concrete, interior,

00:49:59.320 --> 00:50:01.300
with an abstract exterior.

00:50:01.300 --> 00:50:04.810
And of course, the house
itself is perfectly concrete.

00:50:04.810 --> 00:50:06.680
The same is true of my home.

00:50:06.680 --> 00:50:11.680
That's also perfectly concrete,
but in a quite different way.

00:50:11.680 --> 00:50:14.260
If a house is a
brown, wooden house,

00:50:14.260 --> 00:50:17.590
it has a brown exterior
surface, but it doesn't just

00:50:17.590 --> 00:50:19.480
have a wooden exterior.

00:50:19.480 --> 00:50:22.840
It's both a concrete object
of some kind and an abstract

00:50:22.840 --> 00:50:25.570
surface, as well as having
a distinguished interior

00:50:25.570 --> 00:50:27.280
with weird properties.

00:50:27.280 --> 00:50:29.680
Well, proceeding
further, we discover

00:50:29.680 --> 00:50:32.260
that container words,
like, say, house,

00:50:32.260 --> 00:50:34.840
have extremely weird properties.

00:50:34.840 --> 00:50:36.700
Certainly, there
can't be any object

00:50:36.700 --> 00:50:39.280
in the world that has this
combination of properties,

00:50:39.280 --> 00:50:41.620
nor do we believe that there is.

00:50:41.620 --> 00:50:44.020
Rather, a word
like house provides

00:50:44.020 --> 00:50:47.080
a certain quite
complex perspective

00:50:47.080 --> 00:50:50.940
for looking at what we take
to be things in the world.

00:50:50.940 --> 00:50:53.920
Furthermore, these properties
are completely unlearned.

00:50:53.920 --> 00:50:56.360
Hence, they must be universal,
and as far as we know,

00:50:56.360 --> 00:50:57.060
they are.

00:50:57.060 --> 00:50:59.380
They're just part of our nature.

00:50:59.380 --> 00:51:00.370
We didn't learn them.

00:51:00.370 --> 00:51:02.080
We couldn't have learned them.

00:51:02.080 --> 00:51:05.050
They're also largely
unknown and unsuspected.

00:51:05.050 --> 00:51:07.520
Take the most
extensive dictionary

00:51:07.520 --> 00:51:10.840
you like, say, the big
Oxford English Dictionary,

00:51:10.840 --> 00:51:12.850
that you read with
a magnifying glass,

00:51:12.850 --> 00:51:14.650
and you find that
it doesn't dream

00:51:14.650 --> 00:51:16.660
of such somatic properties.

00:51:16.660 --> 00:51:18.460
Rather, what the
dictionary does is

00:51:18.460 --> 00:51:21.430
offer some hints that
allow a person who

00:51:21.430 --> 00:51:24.610
already knows almost everything
to pick out the intended

00:51:24.610 --> 00:51:25.750
concept.

00:51:25.750 --> 00:51:28.090
And the same is true
of words generally.

00:51:28.090 --> 00:51:31.200
And in fact, the same is true
of the sound system of language.

00:51:31.200 --> 00:51:34.480
They are largely known in
advance in all their quite

00:51:34.480 --> 00:51:36.310
remarkable intricacy.

00:51:36.310 --> 00:51:39.310
Hence, they're a universal,
a species property.

00:51:39.310 --> 00:51:41.850
The external environment
may fix some details,

00:51:41.850 --> 00:51:44.260
the way the Oxford
English Dictionary can

00:51:44.260 --> 00:51:46.220
fix a few details.

00:51:46.220 --> 00:51:48.940
But language acquisition,
and in fact, probably

00:51:48.940 --> 00:51:51.910
a good deal of what's
misleadingly called learning,

00:51:51.910 --> 00:51:54.780
is really a kind of
biological growth,

00:51:54.780 --> 00:51:57.280
where you fill in
details in an extremely

00:51:57.280 --> 00:51:59.410
rich, predetermined
structure, that

00:51:59.410 --> 00:52:01.180
just goes back to your nature--

00:52:01.180 --> 00:52:04.300
very much like
physical growth or,

00:52:04.300 --> 00:52:07.840
as I think we should say, other
aspects of physical growth.

00:52:07.840 --> 00:52:09.370
Well, if you look
at the structure

00:52:09.370 --> 00:52:13.030
of more complex expressions,
then these conclusions

00:52:13.030 --> 00:52:14.800
are just reinforced.

00:52:14.800 --> 00:52:17.800
Again, let me take pretty
simple cases to illustrate.

00:52:17.800 --> 00:52:20.590
So take the sentence,
say, John ate an apple,

00:52:20.590 --> 00:52:23.530
and drop out the word
apple, and you get John ate.

00:52:23.530 --> 00:52:25.120
And what that means,
everybody knows,

00:52:25.120 --> 00:52:26.860
is John ate something or other.

00:52:26.860 --> 00:52:30.400
So if you eliminate a
phrase from the sentence,

00:52:30.400 --> 00:52:32.710
you interpret it as
something or other.

00:52:32.710 --> 00:52:34.930
John ate means John
ate something or other,

00:52:34.930 --> 00:52:36.290
more or less.

00:52:36.290 --> 00:52:39.280
Take the sentence, a little
bit longer, but not much--

00:52:39.280 --> 00:52:41.920
John is too stubborn
to talk to Bill.

00:52:41.920 --> 00:52:44.650
Well, that means
John is so stubborn,

00:52:44.650 --> 00:52:46.930
that he won't talk to Bill.

00:52:46.930 --> 00:52:49.000
And just as in the case
of John ate an apple,

00:52:49.000 --> 00:52:51.820
drop the last phrase,
Bill, and we get,

00:52:51.820 --> 00:52:53.920
John is too stubborn to talk to.

00:52:53.920 --> 00:52:57.490
And by analogy, or by induction,
if such a thing existed,

00:52:57.490 --> 00:52:59.480
people ought to
interpret it as meaning

00:52:59.480 --> 00:53:03.160
John is so stubborn, that he
won't talk to someone or other.

00:53:03.160 --> 00:53:04.810
However, it doesn't mean that.

00:53:04.810 --> 00:53:08.440
John is too stubborn to talk
to means John is so stubborn,

00:53:08.440 --> 00:53:11.230
that nobody is going
to talk to him, John.

00:53:11.230 --> 00:53:14.620
Somehow it all inverts
if I drop the last word.

00:53:14.620 --> 00:53:16.870
Suppose you make it a
little more complex.

00:53:16.870 --> 00:53:20.850
John is too stubborn to
expect anyone to talk to Bill.

00:53:20.850 --> 00:53:23.590
Well, that means John-- you
have to think maybe a little bit

00:53:23.590 --> 00:53:27.430
here already, because parsing
is not easy and quick.

00:53:27.430 --> 00:53:30.580
John is too stubborn--
it means John

00:53:30.580 --> 00:53:33.790
is too stubborn for
him, John, to expect

00:53:33.790 --> 00:53:35.970
that anyone will talk to Bill.

00:53:35.970 --> 00:53:37.860
Now I'll drop Bill again.

00:53:37.860 --> 00:53:41.970
Now we get John is too stubborn
to expect anyone to talk to.

00:53:41.970 --> 00:53:43.990
And reflect for a
moment, and you'll

00:53:43.990 --> 00:53:46.510
see that the meaning
shifts radically.

00:53:46.510 --> 00:53:50.140
It means John is so stubborn,
that somebody or other doesn't

00:53:50.140 --> 00:53:52.450
expect anyone to
talk to him, John.

00:53:52.450 --> 00:53:54.310
Everything shifts.

00:53:54.310 --> 00:53:58.230
Now, all of this is inside you.

00:53:58.230 --> 00:53:59.710
Nobody could ever learn it.

00:53:59.710 --> 00:54:01.570
Nobody could possibly learn it.

00:54:01.570 --> 00:54:04.720
None of it is mentioned, even
ever hinted at, in fact, even

00:54:04.720 --> 00:54:07.300
in the most comprehensive
grammar books.

00:54:07.300 --> 00:54:09.040
It's known without experience.

00:54:09.040 --> 00:54:10.570
It's universal,
as far as we know.

00:54:10.570 --> 00:54:12.370
In fact, it better
be universal, or it

00:54:12.370 --> 00:54:14.710
means there are genetic
differences crucially

00:54:14.710 --> 00:54:16.180
among people.

00:54:16.180 --> 00:54:18.850
It just grows out
from our nature.

00:54:18.850 --> 00:54:21.370
A huge variety of
material of that sort

00:54:21.370 --> 00:54:24.430
has come to light in
the last 30 years or so

00:54:24.430 --> 00:54:27.055
as a direct consequence
of something new, namely

00:54:27.055 --> 00:54:31.060
the attempt to actually
discover and to formulate

00:54:31.060 --> 00:54:32.800
the generative
procedures that grow

00:54:32.800 --> 00:54:34.450
in the language
faculty of the brain,

00:54:34.450 --> 00:54:36.250
virtually without experience.

00:54:36.250 --> 00:54:37.640
That had never been done before.

00:54:37.640 --> 00:54:40.820
It was always assumed
that it's all obvious.

00:54:40.820 --> 00:54:42.490
How can it be anything
complicated here?

00:54:42.490 --> 00:54:45.310
If you go back, say,
to the early '50s,

00:54:45.310 --> 00:54:47.770
the problem about language
acquisition was supposed to be,

00:54:47.770 --> 00:54:49.450
why does it take so long?

00:54:49.450 --> 00:54:52.880
Why does it take so long for
such a trivial thing to happen?

00:54:52.880 --> 00:54:56.170
Why do children need so
much exposure to material

00:54:56.170 --> 00:54:59.785
to do this simple process
of habit formation?

00:54:59.785 --> 00:55:01.840
As soon as you try to
describe the facts,

00:55:01.840 --> 00:55:04.160
you discover that it's
quite the opposite.

00:55:04.160 --> 00:55:06.220
There's extremely
intricate things

00:55:06.220 --> 00:55:08.820
that are known even
in the simplest cases.

00:55:08.820 --> 00:55:10.570
There's no possible
way of picking them up

00:55:10.570 --> 00:55:14.285
from experience, and
consequently, they're

00:55:14.285 --> 00:55:14.785
universal.

00:55:14.785 --> 00:55:16.960
They're part of our nature.

00:55:16.960 --> 00:55:20.230
These are just part of
the generative procedures

00:55:20.230 --> 00:55:22.330
that the brain determines.

00:55:22.330 --> 00:55:25.180
Well, what do these generative
procedures look like?

00:55:25.180 --> 00:55:28.490
Apparently, they look
something like this.

00:55:28.490 --> 00:55:32.050
A language can be
looked at as involving

00:55:32.050 --> 00:55:36.770
a computational procedure and
what you can call a lexicon.

00:55:36.770 --> 00:55:40.660
A lexicon is a selection of
concepts that are associated

00:55:40.660 --> 00:55:43.150
with particular sounds.

00:55:43.150 --> 00:55:47.000
The concepts appear to
be pretty much invariant.

00:55:47.000 --> 00:55:49.180
There are things like
house, let's say.

00:55:49.180 --> 00:55:51.980
And they have very
weird properties,

00:55:51.980 --> 00:55:53.890
which means that they
must be invariant,

00:55:53.890 --> 00:55:56.254
because if they have
very weird properties,

00:55:56.254 --> 00:55:57.670
they've got to be
unlearned, which

00:55:57.670 --> 00:55:59.586
means that they're going
to be invariant, just

00:55:59.586 --> 00:56:00.840
part of our nature.

00:56:00.840 --> 00:56:04.750
The sound variety also
seems quite limited.

00:56:04.750 --> 00:56:06.220
On the other hand,
the association

00:56:06.220 --> 00:56:09.430
between a concept and
a sound looks free.

00:56:09.430 --> 00:56:13.120
So you can say tree in English
and [INAUDIBLE] in German,

00:56:13.120 --> 00:56:14.290
and so on.

00:56:14.290 --> 00:56:17.800
So that tells us what one aspect
of language acquisition is.

00:56:17.800 --> 00:56:23.140
It's determining which of
the given set of concepts,

00:56:23.140 --> 00:56:25.270
with all their richness
and complexity,

00:56:25.270 --> 00:56:28.600
is associated with which
of the possible sounds,

00:56:28.600 --> 00:56:30.400
with their limited variety.

00:56:30.400 --> 00:56:32.720
Notice that that's a
pretty trivial task.

00:56:32.720 --> 00:56:35.680
So you can imagine how a
little bit of experience

00:56:35.680 --> 00:56:38.440
could help fix
those associations,

00:56:38.440 --> 00:56:41.020
if the concepts were given,
and the structure of the sound

00:56:41.020 --> 00:56:42.130
was given.

00:56:42.130 --> 00:56:44.800
Well, the lexicon also
contains what are sometimes

00:56:44.800 --> 00:56:47.110
called formal elements,
things like inflections,

00:56:47.110 --> 00:56:50.620
like tense or plural or
case markers of the kind

00:56:50.620 --> 00:56:54.370
that you find, say, in
German or Latin, and so on.

00:56:54.370 --> 00:56:57.400
And languages do appear to
differ quite substantially

00:56:57.400 --> 00:56:58.441
in these respects.

00:56:58.441 --> 00:57:00.190
However, that seems
to be mostly illusion.

00:57:00.190 --> 00:57:03.310
At least, the more we learn,
the more it looks like illusion.

00:57:03.310 --> 00:57:05.320
So for example,
take, say, cases.

00:57:05.320 --> 00:57:06.430
English doesn't have them.

00:57:06.430 --> 00:57:09.010
You've got to memorize them
when you study German and Latin.

00:57:09.010 --> 00:57:10.870
But that looks illusory.

00:57:10.870 --> 00:57:13.960
It appears to be the case that
English has them fine, in fact,

00:57:13.960 --> 00:57:15.449
exactly the way Latin has them.

00:57:15.449 --> 00:57:17.740
It's just that they don't
happen to come out the mouth.

00:57:17.740 --> 00:57:19.320
They're not articulated.

00:57:19.320 --> 00:57:21.900
They're in the internal
mental computation,

00:57:21.900 --> 00:57:26.110
and their effects, which are
quite ramified, are detectable.

00:57:26.110 --> 00:57:28.060
But that part of
the computation just

00:57:28.060 --> 00:57:30.880
doesn't happen to link
up to the vocal tract.

00:57:30.880 --> 00:57:33.550
So the computation
is running along,

00:57:33.550 --> 00:57:36.040
but where it gets
spelled out of sounds,

00:57:36.040 --> 00:57:38.590
it just didn't have
to look at this stuff.

00:57:38.590 --> 00:57:41.830
Well, that's another aspect
of language acquisition, that

00:57:41.830 --> 00:57:45.580
is, to determine what aspects
of the computational system

00:57:45.580 --> 00:57:48.250
receive an external
articulation.

00:57:48.250 --> 00:57:51.580
There are also some differences
among these formal elements

00:57:51.580 --> 00:57:55.575
that also have to be fixed,
but they appear pretty narrow.

00:57:55.575 --> 00:57:56.950
Notice, incidentally,
that if you

00:57:56.950 --> 00:58:00.040
have a very intricate
system with just

00:58:00.040 --> 00:58:02.710
a few possible
differences in it, which

00:58:02.710 --> 00:58:06.760
could be right at the core
somewhere, making those changes

00:58:06.760 --> 00:58:10.555
may produce something that looks
phenomenally quite different.

00:58:10.555 --> 00:58:11.930
Just look at it
from the outside,

00:58:11.930 --> 00:58:13.346
and they look
radically different,

00:58:13.346 --> 00:58:16.180
even though it's basically the
same thing with really only

00:58:16.180 --> 00:58:17.470
trivial differences.

00:58:17.470 --> 00:58:20.800
And that's pretty much what
languages seem to look like.

00:58:20.800 --> 00:58:22.889
Well, what about the
computational system?

00:58:22.889 --> 00:58:23.680
That's the lexicon.

00:58:23.680 --> 00:58:25.870
What about the
computational system?

00:58:25.870 --> 00:58:29.930
It's possible that it's unique,
that is, it's invariant.

00:58:29.930 --> 00:58:31.420
There's just one of them.

00:58:31.420 --> 00:58:34.080
There's nothing at
all to be learned.

00:58:34.080 --> 00:58:36.130
That's not certain, but
it's at least possible,

00:58:36.130 --> 00:58:37.870
in fact plausible.

00:58:37.870 --> 00:58:40.630
If this picture is
on the right track,

00:58:40.630 --> 00:58:42.790
notice we're very
close to concluding

00:58:42.790 --> 00:58:45.280
that there's only
one human language,

00:58:45.280 --> 00:58:47.860
at least in its deeper aspects.

00:58:47.860 --> 00:58:50.650
And incidentally, going back
to this rational Martian

00:58:50.650 --> 00:58:54.430
scientist, that's what he would
have guessed, in first place.

00:58:54.430 --> 00:58:58.180
Otherwise, acquisition of
these highly intricate systems,

00:58:58.180 --> 00:59:00.850
on the basis of
fragmentary experience

00:59:00.850 --> 00:59:02.710
and the uniformity
of the process,

00:59:02.710 --> 00:59:04.429
would just be a miracle.

00:59:04.429 --> 00:59:06.220
So the natural assumption
is, well, there's

00:59:06.220 --> 00:59:09.580
basically only one of them,
and it's fixed in advance.

00:59:09.580 --> 00:59:11.980
If, in fact,
acquisition of language

00:59:11.980 --> 00:59:15.910
is something like a chicken
embryo becoming a chicken

00:59:15.910 --> 00:59:19.220
or humans undergoing
puberty at a certain age,

00:59:19.220 --> 00:59:21.430
then it's at least
within the realm

00:59:21.430 --> 00:59:25.790
of potential scientific
understanding, not a miracle.

00:59:25.790 --> 00:59:27.580
And that's the way
it seems to be.

00:59:27.580 --> 00:59:30.400
The more we learn, the
more it looks like that.

00:59:30.400 --> 00:59:32.710
More precisely,
there does seem to be

00:59:32.710 --> 00:59:37.150
a certain variety of languages,
apparently a finite variety.

00:59:37.150 --> 00:59:39.940
That is, it does seem to be
the-- or it's a good guess now

00:59:39.940 --> 00:59:43.270
that each possible language
that the language faculty makes

00:59:43.270 --> 00:59:45.280
available, each
possible language

00:59:45.280 --> 00:59:49.450
is fixed by answering a
finite number of fairly

00:59:49.450 --> 00:59:51.400
simple questions.

00:59:51.400 --> 00:59:57.280
You can look at the system as
being like a complex, wired up

00:59:57.280 --> 01:00:01.057
thing which is just given, with
a switch box associated with it

01:00:01.057 --> 01:00:02.390
and a finite number of switches.

01:00:02.390 --> 01:00:03.340
You can flip them up.

01:00:03.340 --> 01:00:05.230
You can flip them down.

01:00:05.230 --> 01:00:07.120
You do that on the basis
of the answer to one

01:00:07.120 --> 01:00:08.870
or another of these
questions, and they've

01:00:08.870 --> 01:00:11.830
got to be pretty
simple questions.

01:00:11.830 --> 01:00:13.450
If we could understand
the way that

01:00:13.450 --> 01:00:16.109
works-- bits and pieces of it
are understood-- if we suppose

01:00:16.109 --> 01:00:17.650
we could understand
it, then we ought

01:00:17.650 --> 01:00:20.350
to be able to, say,
deduce Hungarian

01:00:20.350 --> 01:00:24.460
by setting the switches one way
and deduce Swahili by setting

01:00:24.460 --> 01:00:26.860
the switches a different way.

01:00:26.860 --> 01:00:30.740
The questions must be easily
answered for empirical reasons.

01:00:30.740 --> 01:00:34.340
Children do it highly
efficiently and very fast

01:00:34.340 --> 01:00:38.060
and with very little evidence--
pretty much the way they grow.

01:00:38.060 --> 01:00:44.350
It follows that
languages are learnable.

01:00:44.350 --> 01:00:48.010
That is, if this is
correct, it follows

01:00:48.010 --> 01:00:50.500
that, as I mentioned
before, languages, in fact,

01:00:50.500 --> 01:00:52.634
are learnable, which is
an empirical discovery,

01:00:52.634 --> 01:00:53.800
and a pretty surprising one.

01:00:57.280 --> 01:01:00.092
No reason why it would have
had-- no biological reason why

01:01:00.092 --> 01:01:01.300
it would have had to be true.

01:01:01.300 --> 01:01:04.480
But it looks as
though it may be true,

01:01:04.480 --> 01:01:08.350
largely because the
variety is so restricted.

01:01:08.350 --> 01:01:11.170
Well, assuming that
to be true, we now

01:01:11.170 --> 01:01:12.940
have the following situation.

01:01:12.940 --> 01:01:16.270
Languages appear to be
learnable, which is surprising.

01:01:16.270 --> 01:01:18.430
But languages appear
to be unusable,

01:01:18.430 --> 01:01:20.890
which I don't think
is surprising at all.

01:01:20.890 --> 01:01:24.400
What that means is that
only scattered parts of them

01:01:24.400 --> 01:01:24.970
are usable.

01:01:24.970 --> 01:01:26.720
And of course, those
are the parts we use,

01:01:26.720 --> 01:01:28.160
so we don't notice it.

01:01:28.160 --> 01:01:30.400
Now, actually, this
unusability property

01:01:30.400 --> 01:01:33.730
may be somewhat deeper
than what I suggested.

01:01:33.730 --> 01:01:38.500
Since the origins of modern
generative linguistics,

01:01:38.500 --> 01:01:42.370
there have been attempts to show
that the computational system

01:01:42.370 --> 01:01:47.890
is constrained by certain very
general principles of economy,

01:01:47.890 --> 01:01:50.740
which have a kind of
global character to them.

01:01:50.740 --> 01:01:53.430
And there some recent work
that carries these ideas

01:01:53.430 --> 01:01:54.850
a long step forward.

01:01:54.850 --> 01:01:56.300
It's still pretty tentative.

01:01:56.300 --> 01:01:58.630
In fact, it's unpublished.

01:01:58.630 --> 01:02:01.750
But let me sketch some
leading ideas that

01:02:01.750 --> 01:02:04.180
indicate where things might go.

01:02:04.180 --> 01:02:08.980
Suppose we think of a linguistic
expression in something

01:02:08.980 --> 01:02:11.360
like the following way.

01:02:11.360 --> 01:02:14.200
The language faculty
selects a certain array

01:02:14.200 --> 01:02:15.670
of items from the lexicon.

01:02:15.670 --> 01:02:17.290
That's what it's
going to talk about.

01:02:17.290 --> 01:02:19.900
And it begins
computing away, using

01:02:19.900 --> 01:02:21.610
its computational
procedure-- which

01:02:21.610 --> 01:02:25.100
I'm now assuming to be
invariant across languages.

01:02:25.100 --> 01:02:28.090
It computes in parallel,
just picks out these items,

01:02:28.090 --> 01:02:30.940
and goes computing
along with them.

01:02:30.940 --> 01:02:33.725
It occasionally merges
pieces of computation,

01:02:33.725 --> 01:02:35.920
so you bring them
together, then maybe it

01:02:35.920 --> 01:02:39.220
picks something out and starts
going in parallel again.

01:02:39.220 --> 01:02:41.980
At some point in
the computation,

01:02:41.980 --> 01:02:44.590
they've all been merged.

01:02:44.590 --> 01:02:46.900
It just keeps computing.

01:02:46.900 --> 01:02:49.610
It proceeds merrily on
its way, in other words.

01:02:49.610 --> 01:02:52.900
At some point after
they've merged,

01:02:52.900 --> 01:02:54.880
the system, the
language faculty,

01:02:54.880 --> 01:02:58.840
decides to spell it out,
meaning to provide instructions

01:02:58.840 --> 01:03:01.870
to the articulatory
and perceptual system.

01:03:01.870 --> 01:03:04.960
Then the computation
just keeps going on,

01:03:04.960 --> 01:03:06.640
and it ultimately
provides something

01:03:06.640 --> 01:03:09.460
like a representation of
meaning, which is probably

01:03:09.460 --> 01:03:13.960
to be understood as instructions
for other performance systems.

01:03:13.960 --> 01:03:16.630
Well, proceeding in this
way, the computation

01:03:16.630 --> 01:03:21.730
ultimately proceeds to
paired symbolic expressions,

01:03:21.730 --> 01:03:25.510
to paired outputs, instructions
for the articulatory perceptual

01:03:25.510 --> 01:03:29.530
apparatus, on the one hand,
and instructions for language

01:03:29.530 --> 01:03:32.897
use, or called
somatic sometimes.

01:03:32.897 --> 01:03:34.480
So these are the
two, let's call them,

01:03:34.480 --> 01:03:36.850
interface levels of
the language faculty.

01:03:36.850 --> 01:03:38.980
The language faculty
seems to interface

01:03:38.980 --> 01:03:41.710
with other systems of the mind,
performance systems at two

01:03:41.710 --> 01:03:45.490
points, one having to do with
articulation and perception,

01:03:45.490 --> 01:03:50.720
the other having to do with the
things you do with language--

01:03:50.720 --> 01:03:55.771
referring to things, asking
questions, and so on, roughly

01:03:55.771 --> 01:03:56.270
somatic.

01:03:59.920 --> 01:04:01.485
So let's say that's
what happens.

01:04:01.485 --> 01:04:02.880
It looks like it does.

01:04:02.880 --> 01:04:04.860
Now, of these
various computations,

01:04:04.860 --> 01:04:10.140
only certain of them
converge in a certain sense.

01:04:10.140 --> 01:04:14.623
The sense is that the two
kinds of outputs, the phonetic,

01:04:14.623 --> 01:04:17.640
or instructions, the
articulatory perceptual system

01:04:17.640 --> 01:04:22.140
and the somatic, actually yield
interpretable instructions--

01:04:22.140 --> 01:04:23.910
like they might compute
along and end up

01:04:23.910 --> 01:04:25.290
with an output
that doesn't yield

01:04:25.290 --> 01:04:26.940
interpretable instructions.

01:04:26.940 --> 01:04:28.740
In that case, if either
one of them fails,

01:04:28.740 --> 01:04:31.080
we say that it doesn't converge.

01:04:31.080 --> 01:04:34.710
Now, looking just at the
convergent derivations,

01:04:34.710 --> 01:04:36.600
the convergent
computations, those

01:04:36.600 --> 01:04:40.950
that yield interpretable
paired outputs, some of them

01:04:40.950 --> 01:04:43.920
are blocked by the fact
that others of them

01:04:43.920 --> 01:04:45.630
are more economical.

01:04:45.630 --> 01:04:48.330
That is, they involve
less computation.

01:04:48.330 --> 01:04:51.300
Now, here, you've got to
define amount of computation

01:04:51.300 --> 01:04:53.670
in a very special
and precise sense.

01:04:53.670 --> 01:04:55.951
But it's not an unnatural sense.

01:04:55.951 --> 01:04:57.450
Well, if you can
do this, it's going

01:04:57.450 --> 01:04:59.880
to turn out that many
instructions that

01:04:59.880 --> 01:05:02.220
would be perfectly
interpretable just

01:05:02.220 --> 01:05:04.890
can't be constructed
by the mind, at least

01:05:04.890 --> 01:05:08.430
by the language faculty,
because the outputs are blocked

01:05:08.430 --> 01:05:11.970
by more economical derivations.

01:05:11.970 --> 01:05:13.680
What is the linguistic
expression, then?

01:05:13.680 --> 01:05:15.510
Well, the linguistic
expression is

01:05:15.510 --> 01:05:18.600
nothing other than the
optimal realization

01:05:18.600 --> 01:05:21.960
of certain external
interface conditions.

01:05:21.960 --> 01:05:24.786
A language is the setting
of certain switches,

01:05:24.786 --> 01:05:26.160
and an expression
of the language

01:05:26.160 --> 01:05:30.240
is the optimal realization of
universal interface conditions,

01:05:30.240 --> 01:05:31.680
period.

01:05:31.680 --> 01:05:35.460
All the work would now be
done by special properties

01:05:35.460 --> 01:05:39.270
of the nature of computation
and very special notions

01:05:39.270 --> 01:05:42.240
of economy, which do have
a kind of global character.

01:05:42.240 --> 01:05:44.130
They have a kind of a
least effort character,

01:05:44.130 --> 01:05:45.690
but of a global sort.

01:05:45.690 --> 01:05:48.210
Well, it turns out that quite
a variety of strange things

01:05:48.210 --> 01:05:50.730
can be explained in
these ways, in terms

01:05:50.730 --> 01:05:53.670
of a picture of language that
is really rather elegant.

01:05:53.670 --> 01:05:56.850
It's guided by conditions
of economy of derivation,

01:05:56.850 --> 01:06:00.799
with very simple and
straightforward operations.

01:06:00.799 --> 01:06:02.340
They're not only
elegant, but they're

01:06:02.340 --> 01:06:04.974
pretty surprising for
a biological system.

01:06:04.974 --> 01:06:06.390
In fact, these
properties are more

01:06:06.390 --> 01:06:08.490
like the kind, what
one expects to find,

01:06:08.490 --> 01:06:12.450
for quite unexplained reasons,
in the inorganic world.

01:06:12.450 --> 01:06:14.800
Well, these economy
conditions, as I mentioned,

01:06:14.800 --> 01:06:17.120
have a sort of a global
character to them,

01:06:17.120 --> 01:06:18.810
though not entirely.

01:06:18.810 --> 01:06:21.240
They have a tendency
to yield high degrees

01:06:21.240 --> 01:06:24.540
of computational
complexity that would

01:06:24.540 --> 01:06:27.780
render large parts
of language unusable.

01:06:27.780 --> 01:06:31.130
Well, that's not necessarily
an empirical objection.

01:06:31.130 --> 01:06:33.420
We then turn to an
empirical question.

01:06:33.420 --> 01:06:35.650
Are the parts that
are rendered unusable

01:06:35.650 --> 01:06:36.982
the parts that can't be used?

01:06:36.982 --> 01:06:39.190
We know there are plenty of
parts that can't be used.

01:06:39.190 --> 01:06:42.900
So if it turns out
that parts of language

01:06:42.900 --> 01:06:47.030
involve irresolvable
computational complexity,

01:06:47.030 --> 01:06:49.457
irresolvable by a
reasonable device,

01:06:49.457 --> 01:06:51.290
and those are the parts
you can't use, fine.

01:06:51.290 --> 01:06:55.530
That's a positive result,
not a negative result. Well,

01:06:55.530 --> 01:06:58.290
what we might discover
then, if this continues

01:06:58.290 --> 01:07:01.290
to look as promising as I think
it does now, what we might

01:07:01.290 --> 01:07:04.740
discover is that languages are
learnable, because there isn't

01:07:04.740 --> 01:07:08.910
much to learn, that they're
unusable in large measure,

01:07:08.910 --> 01:07:12.330
but that they're surprisingly
beautiful, which is just

01:07:12.330 --> 01:07:15.330
another mystery, if it
turns out to be true.

01:07:15.330 --> 01:07:16.230
Thanks.

01:07:16.230 --> 01:07:19.280
[APPLAUSE]

